{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_8","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'nl -ba docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/REPAIR-DESIGN.md'","aggregated_output":"     1\t# judging-v13 repair: every field's rule beside its field, the checks that refuse right answers, and a $0 blind conformance test (D734)\n     2\t\n     3\tAlex chose one bounded repair, then a $0 blind test (D734; council round 48, D733). This page is the design, and it goes to the council\n     4\tbefore any code. **Out of scope:**\n     5\t- the frozen ruler and the production configuration;\n     6\t- D725's pass lines and D731's harness rules (scoring, journals, packets, verdict);\n     7\t- the system text's judging sentences.\n     8\t\n     9\t**The structural rule of this repair:** a rule code enforces is either held by the schema (an enum, a typed marker) or written in\n    10\tthe schema beside its field. The check never refuses on a rule the request does not state (AGENTS §3, \"Code over prose\"; §6, \"a request\n    11\tnever asks for what a check refuses\"; \"a field's definition sits in the schema beside the field\").\n    12\t\n    13\t## 1. The answer shape: links that failed become enums, id fields say they hold ids\n    14\t\n    15\tEvery field the model filled with prose has a name that reads as content: `outcome`, `reference.figure`, `bridge` and `evidence`. Of\n    16\tthe 90 member answers, 0 named a figure as their outcome. `inputs`, whose content is plainly a list of figures, held prose once in 72\n    17\tanswers.\n    18\t\n    19\t| Today (schema field) | Repaired | Enforced by |\n    20\t|---|---|---|\n    21\t| `answers[].outcome` (free string) | **`figures[].outcome_for`**: an array of member ids (enum). The figure marked for a member is that member's outcome | the schema enum; code checks exactly one marked figure per `forecast` member and none for an `unresolved` one (C7) |\n    22\t| `answers[].reference {figure, unavailable}` | **`figures[].reference_for`**: an array of member ids (enum), plus **`answers[].reference_unavailable`**: nullable string | the schema enum; code checks exactly one of a marked figure or a reason (C5) |\n    23\t| `figures[].evidence` (free string) | **`evidence_id`**: an enum of the request's passage ids, or null | the schema enum (the request already uses this enum for `cites`) |\n    24\t| `figures[].bridge` | **`bridge_estimate_id`**: the id of an estimate in `figures`; the assumption's words go in that estimate's `basis_text` | the description, then C7 and C8 |\n    25\t| `figures[].disputed` | **`disputed_input_id`** | the description, then C8 |\n    26\t| `figures[].converts` | **`converts_input_ids`** | the description, then C8 |\n    27\t| `figures[].adjusts.figure` | **`adjusts.figure_id`** (a figure, never a passage) | the description, then C7 |\n    28\t| `figures[].unit` (free string) | **enum `USD bn`, `USD`, `%`, `x`, `units`** | the schema enum |\n    29\t| `figures[].scenario` (free string; `range` is special in the check) | **enum `base`, `low`, `high`, `range`** | the schema enum |\n    30\t\n    31\t**One owner for the conversion.** `calc_check.from_answer(data)` converts the schema's shape into the shape the checks read, and\n    32\treports the marker errors as C7 and C5. It replaces `strip`/`without_nulls` at both call sites (`paid_gate.Gate.attempt`,\n    33\t`render_v13`). The $0 gate's fixtures stay in the internal shape. The rehearsal's stub answers move to the schema's shape.\n    34\t\n    35\t## 2. Each field's rule, beside the field (descriptions to be added; exact text is set in the build and read in review)\n    36\t\n    37\t| Field | The rule the check holds today | Where the request states it after the repair |\n    38\t|---|---|---|\n    39\t| `id` | unique; referenced by the id fields | \"A short id, unique in this list (for example F1); other fields name figures by it.\" |\n    40\t| `what.period` | `_quarters` reads `FY2027`, `FY2027 Q1`–`Q4`, `FY2027 H1`/`H2`; roll_forward needs `as of <date>` and `end of <period>`; growth periods (§3.2) | a closed list with examples: \"FY2027\", \"FY2027 Q3\", \"FY2027 H1\", \"as of 2026-07-26\", \"end of FY2027\", \"CY2027\", \"FY2027 Q4 vs FY2026 Q4\" (a growth rate) |\n    41\t| `what.measure` | per-unit identity needs `price per unit` and `units`; outcome keys must match exactly | added: \"a per-unit price is measure 'price per unit' (unit USD), a count is 'units'; an outcome uses its member's key exactly\" |\n    42\t| `value`, `low`, `high`, `bound` | exactly one of a value or an ordered range; a bound qualifies a value | \"Either value (with bound when the figure is 'more than', 'about' …) or low and high, never both. '$108.0 billion, plus or minus 2%' is two source figures from one expression, midpoint 108 and relative 2 (%); its range is a computed range_from_relative.\" |\n    43\t| `unit` | `USD bn`, `USD`, `%`, `x`, `units` | the enum, and: \"money in billions of US dollars ($1.05 trillion = 1050; $500 million = 0.5); a percentage as its number (92.5% = 92.5)\" |\n    44\t| `evidence_id`, `expression`, `component` | the expression is copied from the passage's quote and contains one complete numeric expression (§3.4) | \"source only: the passage id; the expression copied word for word from that item's Quote line, never its summary\" |\n    45\t| `inputs` | ids of figures; the operation's arity | \"computed only: ids of figures in this list (never passage ids), in the operation's order\" |\n    46\t| `operation` | arity and meaning (`_compute`) | each operation with its operands: sum(a, b, …) with signs; difference(a, b) = a − b; product(a, b, …) of multipliers (x) or units × price; ratio(a, b) = a / b; growth(old, new) in %; apply_growth(base, growth %); share(whole, share %); range_from_relative(midpoint, relative %); roll_forward(opening, flows …) with signs |\n    47\t| `signs` | sum and roll_forward only, one ±1 per input | \"sum and roll_forward only: one 1 or −1 per input, in order (a − b − c is sum with signs [1, −1, −1]); otherwise null\" |\n    48\t| `relation` | as today | kept, and the disputed operand named by `disputed_input_id` |\n    49\t| `bridge_estimate_id`, `converts_input_ids` | a bridge is an estimate whose `converts` equals the differing operands | \"only when the inputs measure something other than the output and no identity in `operation` covers it: the id of an estimate figure whose converts_input_ids lists exactly those inputs and whose basis_text states the assumption\" |\n    50\t| `basis_text`, `rests_on`, `adjusts` | estimate only | \"estimate only: the assumption in words; the ids of figures and passages it rests on; the figure it changes and how\" |\n    51\t| `outcome_for`, `reference_for` | §1 | \"the member(s) whose forecast this figure is (exactly one per forecast member)\"; \"the member(s) for which this figure is the market's reference: a consensus, guidance or outside figure, or one computed only from those and reported figures\" |\n    52\t| `rung`, `reasons`, `why`, `reference_unavailable` | C6, C5 | \"the rung whose band contains the outcome\"; \"unresolved only: why\"; \"only when no reference figure exists: why\" |\n    53\t\n    54\t## 3. Checks that refuse right answers (each found on a stored answer; each change keeps the refusal it was built for)\n    55\t\n    56\t| # | Stored right answer refused | Today's rule | Change | Still refused |\n    57\t|---|---|---|---|---|\n    58\t| 3.1 | Q1 + Q2 Data Center revenue = \"FY2027 first half\" | `_quarters` reads `H1` only | no check change; the period list is stated (§2) and other labels are refused with a message that names the list | periods that overlap or do not form the output |\n    59\t| 3.2 | FY2026 Q4 × (1 + the source's 65% year-over-year growth) = FY2027 Q4 | `apply_growth` needs a bridge unless the family declares `same_base` | a growth figure states its comparison in its period, \"FY2027 Q4 vs FY2026 Q4\". `apply_growth(base, g)` needs no bridge when base and output are one quantity (population, measure, scope) and g's period is \"<output period> vs <base period>\". The `growth` operation's output takes the same form | a rate applied to another base period (a Q3 sequential rate on FY2026 Q4) without a bridge |\n    60\t| 3.3 | $411 billion × 92.5% computed as 38,017.5 | `product` multiplies the raw number | `product` refuses a `%` operand with \"use share (whole, share %)\"; `share` is described | a percentage multiplied as a plain number |\n    61\t| 3.4 | \"well over $300 billion\", \"65% growth in Q4\" | the expression must equal an extracted expression | the expression must be a verbatim substring of the quote and contain exactly one extracted expression, matched by position; that expression decides value, unit, bound and component | a stripped qualifier (\"$300 billion\" from \"well over $300 billion\"); an expression holding two numbers; an expression not in the quote |\n    62\t| 3.5 | $1.05 trillion compared with bands in billions | free unit string | the unit enum and its description (§2) | — |\n    63\t\n    64\t**Not changed:**\n    65\t- scope changes still need a bridge;\n    66\t- an `overlap_unknown` is recorded, not refused;\n    67\t- cycles are refused;\n    68\t- a summary is not a source;\n    69\t- C3, C4, C9 and C10 are unchanged.\n    70\t\n    71\t## 4. Tables\n    72\t\n    73\t**Value × reader.**\n    74\t- **The schema** (`render_v13.v13_schema`) is read by `paid_gate.item`, which rebuilds the plan (a new pinned plan under a new dated\n    75\t  name; `paid-plan.json` and its run stay as the record), and by `render_v13.main`.\n    76\t- **The converted answer** (`from_answer`) is read by `cc.check`, `cc.publish_decision`, `paid_gate.summarise` (merge), `packets`\n    77\t  (`card_view`, `normalise`) and the verdict.\n    78\t- **The fields renamed in §1** are read by none of these after conversion: the checks keep their internal names.\n    79\t- **`model.check_schema`** validates the enums before any check.\n    80\t\n    81\t**New path × old checks.**\n    82\t- The $0 gate (87 items), the paid rehearsal (63 checks) and both mutant sets run again. The internal-shape fixtures are unchanged.\n    83\t- The stub answers in the schema's shape are rewritten to the new shape.\n    84\t- A new mutant for each change in §3 shows its test fails without it.\n    85\t\n    86\t**State × event (the blind test harness).**\n    87\t\n    88\t| Event | Outcome |\n    89\t|---|---|\n    90\t| a writer's output is not JSON, or fails the schema | recorded as a writer refusal on a stated rule (the schema), with the raw text |\n    91\t| a refusal by the checks | classified (§5) by the sentence of the request that states the rule, or \"not stated\" |\n    92\t| a writer session fails or times out | recorded; resent once; a second failure leaves that request unwritten, and the test is INCOMPLETE |\n    93\t| a later request shares a writer session | never: one fresh session per writer, all six requests in one prompt, answered in order |\n    94\t\n    95\t**Prior decisions.**\n    96\t- D725: lines unchanged.\n    97\t- D731: harness rules unchanged.\n    98\t- D728: no earlier forecast.\n    99\t- D359, D723: no product launcher.\n   100\t- AGENTS §3 and §6.\n   101\t- D733, D734.\n   102\t- DESIGN.md §2 for the internal format; this page changes only the schema's shape and the four rules of §3.\n   103\t\n   104\t## 5. The blind conformance test (registered before it runs; $0, no provider call)\n   105\t\n   106\t**Inputs: fresh requests, not the gate's.**\n   107\t- One joint pair, old-2 v14 call 8620 and v9 call 8621, with its two separate requests.\n   108\t- Three singletons:\n   109\t  - O, old-2 v29 call 8507;\n   110\t  - S, old-3 v6 call 9043;\n   111\t  - E, old-2 v41 call 8287.\n   112\t- Six requests in all, built by the repaired `paid_gate.item` exactly as the gate builds its own.\n   113\t- These calls must differ from the gate's calls, and the build reports how much of their evidence they share with the gate's.\n   114\t\n   115\t**Part A, the checks against registered forms (code).**\n   116\t- **A1, correct forms.** Each is taken from a stored answer whose intended figure and source can be identified, written in the new\n   117\t  shape, converted, and checked inside a minimal family. Each must pass:\n   118\t  - $108.0B ± 2% as midpoint and relative, with range_from_relative;\n   119\t  - Q1 + Q2 = FY2027 H1;\n   120\t  - a year-over-year apply_growth with its \"vs\" period;\n   121\t  - company total × the Data Center mix through share;\n   122\t  - \"well over $300 billion\" and \"65% growth in Q4\" as contained expressions;\n   123\t  - a four-term residual as a sum with signs;\n   124\t  - $1.05 trillion as 1050 USD bn against bands in billions;\n   125\t  - $25 billion as 25 USD bn.\n   126\t- **A2, planted defects on copies of A1.** Each must be refused by its named check:\n   127\t  - 108.5 for $108.0B (C1);\n   128\t  - \"$300 billion\" stripped from \"well over $300 billion\" (C1);\n   129\t  - an expression holding two numbers (C1);\n   130\t  - Q2 + Q3 labelled H1 (C8);\n   131\t  - a Data Center quarter summed into company revenue (C8);\n   132\t  - a percentage through product (C2 or C8, with the share message);\n   133\t  - a growth rate on another base period with no bridge (C8);\n   134\t  - a cycle (C7);\n   135\t  - no outcome marker, and two outcome markers (C7);\n   136\t  - both a reference marker and a reason (C5).\n   137\t- **A3, the existing harness.** The $0 gate, the rehearsal and the mutants pass again.\n   138\t\n   139\t**Part B, the request read by blind writers.**\n   140\t- **Two writers:** a fresh Claude subagent and a fresh read-only Codex session. Each is given only the six requests' system text,\n   141\t  user text and schema as files, and the instruction to answer each as the JSON schema defines it.\n   142\t- **Each writer's first outputs** go through `model.check_schema`, `from_answer` and `cc.check` unedited.\n   143\t- **Every refusal is classified:**\n   144\t  - (i) the request states the rule (the sentence is quoted), so the writer erred;\n   145\t  - (ii) the request does not state it;\n   146\t  - (iii) the check refuses a right answer (read against the passages).\n   147\t\n   148\t**Stop.** The test stops on any of these:\n   149\t- any A1 form refused;\n   150\t- any A2 defect passing its check;\n   151\t- any refusal in Part B classed (ii) or (iii);\n   152\t- an INCOMPLETE run.\n   153\t\n   154\t**Reported beside the verdict:**\n   155\t- how many of the 12 writer answers pass every check;\n   156\t- the (i) refusals by rule.\n   157\t\n   158\tFewer than 6 of 12 passing is not a stop, but the council reconsiders the format's weight before any re-send is asked for. A\n   159\twriter is a stronger reader than gpt-6-luna, so a clean result shows the request states its rules. It does not show luna will\n   160\tfollow them: only the re-send measures that.\n   161\t\n   162\t**A clean result** goes to Alex as a request to re-send the same 36 requests, rebuilt with the repaired request, under a new\n   163\tdecision with D725's lines.\n   164\t\n   165\t**Cost:** $0 at the provider. The writers run as a subagent and a Codex session.\n   166\t\n   167\t**Request size:** the schema's descriptions grow it from 9,624 characters. The growth is the missing definitions, which is the\n   168\twritten reason (AGENTS §6), and the build reports characters before and after. The system text is unchanged.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_7","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '10957,11185p;3711,3728p' docs/DECISIONS-log.md\"","aggregated_output":"## D355 · 2026-11-04 · The filing topic closes without a change: filing is about 85% right; the remaining misses are recorded as known defects\n\n- Alex opened the topic on 2026-11-03 (\"你这个单独开一个专题出来解决 … 是promot，是系统结构设计还是到底哪里有问题挂错一堆东西，机制是不是有问题（一定要挂吗？有没有可能是杂音\"),\n  set the method on 2026-11-04 (\"We need to validate the hypothesis in a very simple way and to make sure that it works on a high level before we\n  dive into do the full analysis stability test\"; \"如果有什么不符合hypothesis 的地方你需要终止然后和我说我们重新评估\"), and closed it: \"那就1吧\"\n  (option 1: close the topic, record the pattern as a known defect, go to step 5).\n- What was found (`research/2026-11-03-filing/report.html`, `log.md` rows 1–17): three rounds of Claude–Codex review converged on \"the question\n  definition is too narrow\"; an independent challenge and the first stored-request test refuted it (the earlier version's definition was already\n  broad; the same request gives different filings from send to send). Two cheap tests of fixes did not fit their hypotheses and stopped: removing the\n  total-revenue fallback alone leaves good indirect evidence unlinked; teaching \"one fact, two uses\" made the model add a second company-revenue\n  question instead. A size measurement per class (six observations per fact) and a wider check before test 3 put filing at about 85% right:\n  the company's own quarterly figures 82%, forecasts 91%, other companies' figures 84% on fourteen facts.\n- Known defects, recorded and not fixed: (1) competitor and customer news (a cloud provider's backlog, a rival's customer forecasts, a chip\n  designer's revenue) is filed under the company's revenue questions instead of the share, capital-spending or custom-chip question it informs;\n  the model reads it as \"what it means for the company\" and the fallback sentence sends it there; the custom-chip question lost its main evidence\n  this way in the frozen draws. (2) Share facts are often not written at all (the gold's core \"China share fallen to zero\" in none of six\n  observations): an extraction recall miss, not filing. (3) The model files only relations an article states; one needing industry knowledge (a\n  chip designer's AI revenue as a measure of cloud providers' custom-chip spending) is not made unless the article says it.\n## D725 · 2026-11-22 · Council round 45/45b: the number answers fail because related numbers have no shared, checked calculation; one judge version and one test recommended to Alex\n\n- **Alex's words** (2026-11-22): \"work with counsel and have a look at the traces of this problem … what is wrong with the system design\n  overall … look at the code, look at the traces, deeply battle it out with counsel … because I think it's the most important thing\n  that we don't hallucinate with the numbers, and I want you to get this right\".\n- **The record:** `docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/` (`RESULT.md`).\n  - Claude's reflection was written before Codex's.\n  - Codex ran an independent investigation (fresh thread `01a20501…`) and a challenge round.\n  - Consensus. One wording point stays open: the $146B is arithmetically a residual, but the order of the judge's choices is unknown.\n- **Diagnosis:**\n  1. Answers are prose; code ties no figure to a source and recomputes nothing. The production check passes contradictory or invented\n     figures.\n  2. Related questions compute the same quantities separately. The unit is one question, with 0 dependencies.\n  3. Filing and the evidence in view split their inputs. The previous conclusion carries figures without provenance.\n  4. Opening's bands amplify an error.\n- **Measured:**\n  - Data Center Q4 above company Q4 on at least 13 of 26 days in old-1;\n  - Data Center's full year above the total's on 20 consecutive days in old-2;\n  - different Q3 bases on 17 of 18 paired days;\n  - shared evidence between total and Data Center 8% in old-2;\n  - 17 of the 24 double-rejected answers from unchecked arithmetic.\n- **Recommended design** (a new judge version, Alex's to open):\n  - one judging call per related family, each question keeping its answer from one calculation;\n  - figures labelled by the judge (source, calculation, assumption; scope and period);\n  - code checks only identity and arithmetic (figure in quote, recompute, part within whole, one value per shared quantity, a sourced\n    reference);\n  - unreconciled forecasts shown as unresolved;\n  - extraction unchanged;\n  - families declared for the test.\n- **Interim for a first release:** number questions shown as \"Forecast unavailable\" until they pass the checks.\n- **The test:** a $0 gate (hand reconstructions of five stored cases, four gold controls, three planted errors), then a paid gate\n  (36 calls; joint against separate on the three draws' 09-20 pair, plus O, S and E singletons). Pass and stop lines are in `RESULT.md`.\n- **Not done:** nothing built, sent or changed.\n\n## D726 · 2026-11-22 · Alex opens a judge version for related number questions ($0 gate first) and agrees to \"Forecast unavailable\" as the first-release interim\n\n- **The questions** (after D725):\n  - \"要不要开一个新的判官版本来试修法（相关的题合在一次里算，每个数字标明出处，程序检查算术和出处）？\" Options: 开，先做 0 元那一关\n    (recommended: the format and checks designed and through the council, then the $0 gate on five stored cases, four gold controls and\n    three planted errors; the paid gate asks Alex for its ceiling); 先不开.\n  - \"第一版上线前的过渡：数字题只显示问题和来源，档位和预测文字写成'预测暂缺'，等通过检查再放出来。你同意这个做法吗？\" Options: 同意\n    (recommended); 暂不定.\n- **Alex's answers** (2026-11-22): \"开，先做 0 元那一关 (推荐)\"; \"同意 (推荐)\".\n- **Decided (Alex):**\n  - A new judge version is opened for the bounded test of D725: related number questions judged together, with labelled figures and\n    code checks of identity and arithmetic. It is designed and reviewed at $0, then the $0 gate runs. The paid gate needs Alex's\n    ceiling.\n  - Until the number answers pass, a first release shows number questions with their sources and \"Forecast unavailable\": the band, the\n    forecast text and its history withheld together. It is built with the release work, which is not started.\n- poc-v118 stays the product.\n\n## D727 · 2026-11-22 · judging-v13's $0 gate: the design passed council (round 46e); gate runs 1–6 and council rounds 46f–46j; the gate is not yet passed; one choice goes to Alex\n\n- **Design:** `docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/DESIGN.md`, v1 to v9. Council rounds 46 to 46e reached READY FOR THE $0 GATE on v5.\n  - **v3's structural rule:** code derives every relation it checks from the calculation graph and the passage text.\n- **The gate** (`gate.py`, `calc_check.py`, `mutants.py`, `render_v13.py`, `judging-v13-draft.txt`, `REQUEST-AUDIT.md`), all harness-only\n  and $0.\n  - **Run 1 failed** on the gold's q19 (revenue over a per-unit price). The per-unit identity was added.\n  - **Runs 2 to 6** pass the registered items, the fixtures growing from 29 to 41 as each council round added variants.\n  - **The mutants:** 15 of 15 are caught, and the unmutated control passes.\n  - **The rendered request:** one stored family (old-2 at 09-20) is rendered: system text 4,835 characters (v7 4,924), user message\n    123,304 characters, 237 evidence items. A conforming answer passes the schema and the checks.\n- **What it established:**\n  - each of the five stored defects is caught by its named check:\n    - a segment quarter above the company quarter;\n    - two Q3 bases;\n    - an unknown-overlap addition shown with its $35B alternate;\n    - a scope conversion with no stated assumption;\n    - a trim across $40B;\n  - the self-made $414B reference is refused;\n  - the four gold reasonings (q01, q02, q19, q22) are expressible.\n- **Council rounds 46f to 46j, each GATE FAIL:** every round found a variant by which a model could relabel or route around a\n  structural check. The two recurring mechanisms were answered with one structural rule each in v9:\n  - scenario membership derived along the whole path;\n  - earlier-forecast status derived from the basis or a pointer.\n- **Open after round 46j** (`codex-r46j.md`):\n  1. scenario names can still separate a part from its whole; related outcomes must cover the same scenarios;\n  2. the range rule refuses q22's reuse of q01's range; C9 must work per scenario;\n  3. an earlier forecast restated as a fresh estimate is invisible to code.\n\n  Codex proposes leaving the earlier forecasts out of the calculation request and comparing after. That changes what the judge sees\n  (D198: the previous conclusion carries the pieces not shown), so it goes to Alex.\n- **Not done:** nothing sent; no product file changed; the paid gate not asked.\n\n## D728 · 2026-11-22 · Alex: the family judge does not see its earlier forecasts while calculating\n\n- **The question** (after D727): \"新判官算数的时候，要不要让它看到自己上一次的预测？… 不给看的代价：以前靠'上次结论'带着的那些老证据（这次没展示的）就丢了，只剩最近被引用过的和新到的证据。\"\n  Options:\n  - 算的时候不给看 (recommended: compute from the evidence alone, then compare with the earlier conclusion; the old pieces' carried\n    effect is lost and is measured in the paid test);\n  - 照旧给看.\n- **Alex's answer** (2026-11-22): \"算的时候不给看 (推荐)\".\n- **Decided (Alex), for judging-v13:**\n  - the calculation request shows no earlier forecast;\n  - whether a member's conclusion changed is derived by code afterwards, from its earlier rung, outcome and source figures;\n  - the evidence in view stays D198's arrivals and basis.\n- **D198 for judging-v7 is unchanged.**\n\n## D729 · 2026-11-22 · judging-v13's $0 gate passes (council round 46ad); the paid gate needs its harness built at $0 and Alex's ceiling\n\n- **Verdict:** GATE PASS STANDS (`docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/codex-r46ad.md`, `GATE-RESULT.md`).\n  - Gate run 26: 87 of 87, identical under three hash seeds.\n  - Mutants: 47 of 47 caught.\n  - One stored family is rendered (system text 4,373 characters), and a conforming answer passes the schema and every check.\n- **The path:** design v1 to v10, then council rounds 46 to 46ad.\n  - Run 1 failed on the gold's q19 and the per-unit identity was added.\n  - Each later council finding was answered and kept as a gate item.\n  - The structural rules (relations derived from the graph and the passage, scenarios derived along the path, earlier-forecast status\n    from the basis, one recomputed result for checks and publication, bounds carried through arithmetic) are recorded in\n    GATE-RESULT.md.\n- **Not shown:** how a model uses the format (the paid gate, D725), production lifecycle, the product request builder.\n- **Next:**\n  1. the paid gate's sender and blind reading packets, built and rehearsed at $0 and reviewed by the council;\n  2. Alex's ceiling for the 36 calls, estimated at $0.2–0.4.\n- **Not done:** nothing sent; no product file changed; poc-v118 stays the product.\n\n## D730 · 2026-11-22 · Alex: build the paid gate's harness at $0, then ask for the ceiling\n\n- **The question** (after D729): \"要不要现在接着搭花钱测试用的发送程序和盲审材料？搭的过程不花钱，搭好、演练、过 council 之后再问你上限（预计 36 次调用，0.2–0.4 美元）。\"\n  Options:\n  - 接着搭，搭好再问上限 (recommended);\n  - 先停在这里.\n- **Alex's answer** (2026-11-22): \"接着搭，搭好再问上限 (推荐)\".\n- **Decided (Alex):** the sender and the blind reading packets for D725's paid gate are built, rehearsed at $0 and reviewed by the\n  council. Nothing is sent until Alex names the ceiling.\n\n## D731 · 2026-11-22 · judging-v13's paid-gate harness is ready to send (council round 47h); the first attempt is scored on both arms; the ceiling is Alex's\n\n- **What was built (D730):** `research/2026-11-22-number-answers/paid_gate.py` (plan, send, recover, packets, verdict, replay),\n  `paid_rehearsal.py` (the provider stubbed at urllib beneath the real budget transport), `paid_mutants.py`; the plan is pinned\n  (`paid-plan.sha256`, 6f81dae5…). Record: `PAID-GATE.md` §3 to §3h.\n- **Council rounds 47 to 47h** (Codex thread `01a20501…`, read-only): each round's findings were checked against the code and fixed\n  with a rehearsal case and a mutant; round 47h answered \"READY TO SEND (once Alex names the ceiling)\".\n- **Decided by the council (D725's lines unchanged):**\n  - The first attempt is scored on both arms. D725 registers the arms \"under the same format and checks\" and says nothing about\n    retries. A joint retry can repair a cross-card conflict that the separate arm sees only after both calls end, so scoring the\n    final answers would credit the joint arm with a second chance the separate arm cannot have. Retries are sent after every first\n    attempt and read for diagnosis only (`attempts.md`, `retries.jsonl`).\n  - The readers' packets hold only what their four questions need, per card the scored answer's passages, figures, recomputed\n    outcome, assumptions and card. No attempt history, code decision or check code (a cross-member code would reveal the arm).\n  - The text the readers receive commits to the packet-to-unit mapping: a salted key's sha256 is its first line. The verdict renders\n    every packet again from the run and refuses any mismatch.\n  - The separate arm's two answers are reconciled one to one: a declared shared forecast is unified only when each call holds exactly\n    one, with the same value, bounds and unit.\n  - Completeness is judged on the scored attempts. The readings are sealed into the run, the verdict is written once, and `replay`\n    must reproduce it.\n- **Checks:** rehearsal 63 of 63 (three runs); mutants 33 of 33 caught, the control passes; the $0 gate unchanged (87 of 87, gate run 27).\n- **Cost, at `hivemind.model.PRICES` (gpt-6-luna $0.10 input, $0.50 output per million tokens; tokens estimated as characters / 4):**\n  - the 36 first attempts are about $0.17 at 4,000 output tokens each, and at most $0.39 if every answer used its 16,000-token cap;\n  - the budget transport reserves at most $0.039 for one request before sending;\n  - so $0.50 covers every scored attempt. Retries come last, and a ceiling stop among them leaves the scored test complete.\n- **Next:** Alex names the ceiling (proposed $0.50). Nothing has been sent.\n\n## D732 · 2026-11-22 · Alex: the paid gate's ceiling is $0.50\n\n- **The question** (after D731): \"judging-v13 花钱那一关（36 次计分请求，按公开价格约 0.17 美元，最坏 0.39 美元；重试排在最后、只作诊断）已经过理事会，现在可以发。你给多少上限？\"\n  Options:\n  - 0.5 美元 (recommended);\n  - 0.8 美元;\n  - 先不发.\n- **Alex's answer** (2026-11-22): \"0.5 美元 (推荐)\".\n- **Decided (Alex):** `paid_gate.py`'s `APPROVED_CEILING` is 0.5. The gate is sent once, from an empty folder\n  (`poc/data/judging-v13-paid-gate-2026-11-22/`), detached with a log and pid file, after a machine and network check. It is scored by\n  `PAID-GATE.md` §4 on the first attempts.\n\n## D733 · 2026-11-22 · judging-v13's paid gate: STOP, every answer refused; the format's rules were never stated in the request (council round 48); repair or close is Alex's choice\n\n- **What ran** (D732): 36 scored first attempts and 36 diagnostic retries, gpt-6-luna, $0.3677 of $0.50 (ledger: 72 attempts,\n  not stopped). Run folder `poc/data/judging-v13-paid-gate-2026-11-22/`. Record: `research/2026-11-22-number-answers/PAID-GATE-RESULT.md`.\n- **Registered result (D725's lines, scored as written, D680):** all 72 answers refused by `calc_check.py`, none published; the joint\n  arm is usable in 0 of 9 pairs (needs 8). **STOP.** Complete, not INCOMPLETE: every attempt finished. The blind reading was not run:\n  its brief says a card with no answer cannot be usable, and every card has none.\n- **What it measures:** not D725's question (whether one call over a family gives better numbers), only that the answer format, as\n  the request stated it, could not be filled.\n- **Census (council round 48; Codex reproduced every refusal):**\n  - 0 of 90 member answers named a figure as the outcome; the model wrote the number in words (\"$398.1 billion\");\n  - prose where an id is expected, first attempts: `reference.figure` 39, `evidence` 175 (139 on source figures), `bridge` 49;\n  - 52 figures gave both a value and a range;\n  - the structure check stopped 63 of 72 answers before any source or arithmetic check.\n- **Mechanism:**\n  - The format's rules live in the check, not in the request. The sent schema has 7 field descriptions. `evidence`, `outcome`,\n    `unit`, `value`/`low`/`high`, `inputs`, `operation`, `signs` and ten more have none.\n  - The descriptions of `bridge` and `reference` read as asking for prose, while the check reads ids.\n  - Under that layer, checks refuse right answers:\n    - Q1 + Q2 = \"first half\" is refused (the parser reads only `H1`);\n    - a source-stated year-over-year growth applied to last year's quarter needs a bridge;\n    - $411 billion × 92.5% is computed by `product` as 38,017.5;\n    - \"well over $300 billion\" is not matched;\n    - trillions are compared with billions.\n  - Model errors in the sample: a growth rate supported by forecasts computed from it (a cycle), and two commitments added without\n    an established overlap.\n- **Process error (Claude's):**\n  - The $0 gate's passing answer was written knowing the check's ids, units and spellings. It proved the harness, not the request.\n    The missed step is an answer written by a reader who sees only the outgoing request, run through the real checks (AGENTS §6,\n    \"Check before the send\").\n  - Council rounds 47–47h reviewed the harness and never the request against the check.\n  - The mid-run diagnosis named the wrong field (`rests_on`).\n- **Council consensus, round 48** (`claude-r48.md`, `codex-r48.md`):\n  - Keep the STOP. One recommendation for Alex: a bounded repair of judging-v13's request and checks.\n    - Each field's rule goes beside the field, with enums for ids, units and choices where the schema can hold them.\n    - The demonstrated period, arithmetic, parser and unit failures are fixed. The checks for scope changes, unstated assumptions\n      and cycles stay.\n  - The test is $0: a registered blind conformance test.\n    - Correct examples and planted defects are fixed first. An independent writer sees only the new request, and their first\n      outputs go through the real harness unedited.\n    - The test stops if a correct form is refused, a planted defect passes, or the writer needs an unstated rule.\n    - Only then is a re-send of the same 36 requests asked for, under a new decision with D725's lines unchanged.\n  - Claude's lean, a code-built catalogue of source expressions, was set aside: parser omissions would become missing evidence, and\n    the catalogue leaves the outcome link untouched.\n  - The alternative is to close judging-v13. poc-v118 stays the product (D690), and the number answers stay a known defect.\n- **Next:** Alex chooses between the repair and closing. Nothing is changed or sent before his answer.\n\n## D734 · 2026-11-22 · Alex: repair judging-v13's request and checks once, then a $0 blind conformance test\n\n- **The question** (after D733): \"判数字的新版本（一次判一组相关的数）花钱那一关没过：72 个答案全被挡在“填格式”这一步。原因是填写规则只写在检查代码里、没写进给模型的说明，而且检查代码自己也会把对的判错。这没回答“一起判会不会更准”。下一步怎么走？\"\n  Options:\n  - 修一次再零成本盲测 (recommended);\n  - 关掉这条线.\n- **Alex's answer** (2026-11-22): \"修一次再零成本盲测 (推荐)\".\n- **Decided (Alex):** one bounded repair of judging-v13's request and checks, per council round 48 (D733).\n  - Each field's rule goes beside its field, with enums where the schema can hold them.\n  - The demonstrated check failures are fixed, and the checks for scope changes, unstated assumptions and cycles are kept.\n  - Then a registered $0 blind conformance test, with its stop conditions. Nothing is paid or sent.\n  - A clean result goes back to Alex as a request to re-send the same 36 requests under a new decision, with D725's lines\n    unchanged. A stop goes back to him with its census.\n- **The order of the work (AGENTS §2, §7.3):** the repair design with its tables, then council rounds until one finds nothing\n  serious, then code with tests and mutants. The test is registered before it runs, with its examples fixed first.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_9","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'nl -ba docs/prd/10-filing-phase.md'","aggregated_output":"     1\t# Core PRD for the next phase: filing must recognise indirect relations the way an analyst does\n     2\t\n     3\t> Status: in progress (opened 2026-11-04). Alex: \"你把这个写好成下一阶段核心PRD然后不要drift, 把所有需要的context 全部充分记录，每次开始前都需要revisit, 然后开始做\".\n     4\t> This page is the single source for this phase. In a conflict: Alex's words → `docs/DECISIONS.md` → this page → the other design documents. This page's\n     5\t> conclusions, evidence, constraints, measuring method and process are not rewritten to follow test results;\n     6\t> test results go into `docs/research/2026-11-04-filing-end-to-end/log.md`, and changing this page first needs Alex's word.\n     7\t> Amended 2026-11-04 (Alex replied \"go\" to the proposed amendment; D360): §5.2 drops \"also file under the other party's question\" and puts two\n     8\t> sentences in its place, one on choosing and one on splitting; §6's decision rule is rewritten. How it came about:\n     9\t> `docs/research/2026-11-04-filing-end-to-end/prd-amendment-proposal.md` and `log.md` rows 11–16.\n    10\t> 2026-11-05 (D368): translated into English on Alex's word (\"不，用英文吧\"); §4 corrected to name the frozen standard's version 5 and §5 given a status line, on his delegation (\"你要来帮我看到底做不做\").\n    11\t\n    12\t## 0. Checklist before every start (Alex: \"每次开始前都需要revisit\")\n    13\t\n    14\tBefore starting any step, finish these in order; if one cannot be done, do not start:\n    15\t\n    16\t1. Read §§1–8 of this page in full; read the last three rows of `log.md`, and confirm where the previous step stopped and why.\n    17\t2. Check against §3, \"not in scope\": what this step does is not in that table. If it is in the table, stop and take it to Alex.\n    18\t3. Check against §4, \"constraints\": this step changes nothing frozen; a change to the frozen extraction prompt is the one place §5 names, and Alex's\n    19\t   word for it is already given.\n    20\t4. If this step costs money: its predictions and stop conditions are written down and frozen as §7 says (with their sha256 recorded beside them); the\n    21\t   requests are rebuilt byte for byte as §6 says; the control is re-sent the same day.\n    22\t5. What goes to Alex: one conclusion, one recommendation, one test, after §7's rounds of review; no menu of options; in plain words (the opening of\n    23\t   AGENTS, \"Explain and answer as to a sixteen-year-old\").\n    24\t\n    25\t## 1. Goal (end to end)\n    26\t\n    27\tEach question's judgement must receive the facts that can change its answer; facts that no question receives, those that bear on no question, must\n    28\tnot be piled into some one question. What is measured is the final judgement (the frozen judgement ruler, reader B, at the window end 2026-07-14), not\n    29\tany one step's own score.\n    30\t\n    31\t## 2. The core blocker (proven)\n    32\t\n    33\t**The system uses a fact for a question only when the fact \"states exactly this question's own figure\". An analyst also reasons onward from a fact: the other party's figure in a deal, price and volume for a product's revenue, a product's variants for its launch, and so on. The filing step cannot do this; the judging step reasons onward once it has the facts.**\n    34\t\n    35\tThis one blocker has two faces (described earlier as \"two problems\"):\n    36\t\n    37\t- **The parking lot.** Filing does not recognise facts that bear on a question indirectly, so no question takes them; yet to be kept, a fact must\n    38\t  first be filed (D209, D264). So D273 added the fallback sentence, which parks them in NVIDIA's total-revenue question. Of the reports under the\n    39\t  total-revenue question, in the part the gold read, 32–39% are ones the standard does not keep, 31–44% are kept but used by none of the 22 questions,\n    40\t  and only 7–10% are what the gold actually uses to judge total revenue (three draws); 24–44% of the total-revenue question's judgement citations are\n    41\t  news about other companies or the whole market (Intel graphics-card retail prices, a gas-fired power plant in Taiwan).\n    42\t- **Narrow questions starve.** Questions that only indirect facts can answer do not receive them: the custom-chip question received none of Broadcom's\n    43\t  figures in any of the three draws, and judged \"below $40 billion\" in all three; the operating-margin and NVIDIA capital-spending questions received\n    44\t  nothing at all and were never judged.\n    45\t\n    46\t**Evidence:**\n    47\t\n    48\t| Evidence | Content | Source |\n    49\t|---|---|---|\n    50\t| Filing recognises only \"its own figure\" | The model says \"Broadcom's AI chip revenue is not cloud providers' spending on custom chips\" and \"Google Cloud's backlog is not capital spending\"; on a neutral refiling (no question id named, the rule not shown), 23 of 42 facts that matter are still not filed | `probe-filing-account-d1.md`, `refile-no_fallback.json` |\n    51\t| The judgement reasons onward once it has the facts | The custom-chip question's request of 7-14, sent 3 times in each of the three draws: as it was, 9/9 \"below $40 billion\"; with the Broadcom, Trainium and other facts written in that draw's ledger added, 9/9 left the lowest rung (6 times $40–60 billion, 3 times $60–80 billion), reasoning as the two readers did (\"guidance $16 billion, about $64 billion annualised, less networking chips\") | `predictions-one-test.md`, `one-test-result.json`, `log.md` row 8 |\n    52\t| Where the parking lot came from | D273: with the fallback sentence added, the core items on the extraction ruler went 64 → 68; the extraction ruler does not look at which question a fact is filed under (AGENTS §7.1, \"What it does not measure\") | `docs/DECISIONS.md` D273 |\n    53\t| What the parking lot holds | The figures above; an independent reader on 12 random articles: of 25 misfilings as it was, 21 were parked in the revenue question; 8 and 5 with the fallback deleted | `sink_by_standard.py`, the independent reader's report (`log.md` row 8) |\n    54\t\n    55\tAll files above are in `docs/research/2026-11-04-filing-end-to-end/` unless noted otherwise.\n    56\t\n    57\t## 3. Ruled out or secondary (outside this phase's scope, each recorded separately)\n    58\t\n    59\t| Matter | Conclusion | Source |\n    60\t|---|---|---|\n    61\t| The argument card | Not built; its code is deleted (Alex: \"好，删掉卡片代码\") | `docs/research/2026-11-04-argument-record/log.md` rows 4–11 |\n    62\t| The arrows of the question graph | Really wired backwards: a seed question's arrow means \"supports\" (D208), while the judging code reads it as \"whose conclusion to read\" (PRD 06). As a result themes are judged only after the total-revenue question, so the total-revenue question reads the themes' conclusions of the previous day, and in draw 3 a chain of questions went wrong in turn. But it does not explain the misfiling (filing comes before judging), and the gold itself judges data-center revenue as \"total revenue × 92%\". A topic of its own; settled by D368 (item 4: the arrow means \"supports\"), its fix to be built and measured | `core-problem.md`, `review-core-codex.md` |\n    63\t| Code removes the total-revenue question's own figures | True (\"keep only the narrowest question\" takes a seed arrow for \"a narrower sub-question\"), but each draw it costs the total-revenue question only 0–2 of the facts it needs | `log.md` row 8 |\n    64\t| A filing re-check added at the end | Recovers some, and misfiles about as many (independent reader: about one misfiled for each one filed right); not the solution | The independent reader's report |\n    65\t| One general sentence, \"the other party's figure in a deal counts too\" | The custom-chip question gains, but it pulls Morgan Stanley's price for a Rubin rack from the Rubin revenue question to the capital-spending question, and leaves some facts with no home | `answers.json` (the two arms definition and definition_no_fallback), `measure-corrected.txt` |\n    66\t| Tightening D259's \"leeway\" | Alex's own decision; this phase does not overturn it | D259 |\n    67\t| Opening questions (the Ohio guarantee was not opened); the two readers' disagreement on the China-share question | Another class of problem, not in this phase | `docs/research/2026-11-04-filing-end-to-end/diagnosis.md` §0 |\n    68\t| Model drift | With the same requests, today about half as many facts go into the total-revenue question as on 11-03; the cause is not proven. Compare only with a control re-sent the same day, never with the archive | `log.md` row 6 |\n    69\t\n    70\t## 4. Constraints (what must not move, and why)\n    71\t\n    72\t- **The frozen ruler** (AGENTS §7.1, D261, D262): the extraction standard, version 5 (the signed version 3 with D248's seven boundaries and D250's four\n    73\t  evidence boundaries and counting unit), golden-v5, the seed questions and the judgement gold; not a word changes; a disagreement is a gap in the\n    74\t  system (D263).\n    75\t- **The frozen extraction prompt** (D274): v74i (`poc-v108`) does not change by a word; what this phase changes is the one place that holds the filing\n    76\t  rule (the description of the `links` field), made into a new version with a new configuration and compared with v74i. Alex has given his word for this\n    77\t  work (2026-11-04: \"好你去解决问题\", \"开始做\"); making it the default needs his word again.\n    78\t- **Frozen opening and judging** (D354): this phase does not touch them.\n    79\t- **The standard's text** (in translation): §6, \"file under the narrowest open question; when no question takes it, record no home and drop it, and read\n    80\t  each one at evaluation\"; §5.1, \"makes the answer to some question more or less likely\", with four paths, \"demand, supply, competition, pricing\"; §5.3,\n    81\t  \"another company's matter is not kept when one cannot say which question about the covered company it moves through a path such as demand, supply,\n    82\t  competition or pricing\".\n    83\t- **Decisions**: D207 (one home per fact, the narrowest question), D209 (no home means dropped, each one read), D259 (leeway: a figure question takes\n    84\t  \"the demand, supply, competition and pricing that make it more or less likely\"; the total-revenue question taking more is an accepted cost, \"bounded by\n    85\t  the narrowest-question rule and the driver questions\"), D265 (code keeps only the narrowest), D273 (the fallback sentence), D355 (the two failed tests\n    86\t  of the previous filing topic).\n    87\t- **Prompt principles** (AGENTS §6): carry only the standard and add no new rule; a general hedge-fund analyst: every rule and example reads the same\n    88\t  for a bank, a drug maker, a retailer or a chip company, names neither NVIDIA nor its products or figures, and is not taken from a test article; a\n    89\t  classification is written as a closed set with two or three examples per class, replacing the abstract wording; quality is not fixed by lengthening\n    90\t  the prompt: remove as much as is added, or write down the reason.\n    91\t- **Code decides only \"is it the same thing\", never \"does it matter\"** (AGENTS §2).\n    92\t\n    93\t## 5. What to do\n    94\t\n    95\tStatus: the links-rule version that carried items 1–3 (v74l) failed its one test (§8 step 5); D361 keeps the fallback sentence, so item 3 is not carried out. The plan below is kept as written.\n    96\t\n    97\t1. **Replace the abstract sentence \"makes the answer more or less likely through demand, supply, competition or pricing\" with the few classes in which an analyst recognises an indirect relation, written as a closed set, each class with two or three examples from other industries** (AGENTS §6: one\n    98\t   abstract sentence is a coin flip on every send; examples replace it). A first draft of the classes (checked against the standard line by line at\n    99\t   design time; it holds only meanings the standard already has):\n   100\t   - The question's own figure: this question's figures, guidance, estimates and revisions (estimates for another period or another population follow\n   101\t     the existing rule).\n   102\t   - The other side of the same money: a seller's revenue from the buyers the question counts; those buyers' orders and backlog with these sellers.\n   103\t   - Price and volume: a product's price or shipments, for the question on that product's revenue.\n   104\t   - Variants and parts: a product's variants, alternatives, key parts and schedule changes, for the question on that product's launch.\n   105\t   - Demand, supply and competition moves with a number: customers' orders and capital-spending plans, suppliers' capacity and prices, competitors'\n   106\t     sales and share, for the question they affect.\n   107\t2. **State how to file a fact when two specific questions both bear on it** (the problem the `definition_no_fallback` arm exposed: Morgan Stanley's rack\n   108\t   price was filed under the buyers' capital-spending question, and the seller's Rubin revenue question lost it). One home per fact (D207): file it under\n   109\t   the question its figure measures most directly (for example, a hotel's room rate goes under its room-revenue question, not under the\n   110\t   travellers'-spending question; a parts maker's revenue from carmakers goes under the carmakers' parts-spending question, not under a rival parts\n   111\t   maker's share question); when one sentence gives a separate figure for each of two questions, write two items, each filed under its own question\n   112\t   (code stores two items that quote the same sentence as one record linked to both questions); never also file it under a broader question about the\n   113\t   same company (the failure of D355's test 2: the model filed it under an NVIDIA revenue question as well). The original draft's \"when the other\n   114\t   party's question is open … file it there as well\" conflicted with D207 and was deleted on 2026-11-04 with Alex's agreement (D360).\n   115\t3. **Delete the fallback sentence** and return to the standard's §6: when no question takes a fact, it is not filed; code drops it as \"no home\" and\n   116\t   records it (D209), and evaluation reads each one.\n   117\t4. Change only the one place that holds the filing rule; report its length in characters before and after, and write down the reason for any growth\n   118\t   (this time's reason: examples replace one abstract sentence).\n   119\t\n   120\t## 6. How to measure (the ruler does not move; the method does not change again)\n   121\t\n   122\t- **Requests**: rebuild each stored request byte for byte with the production code, changing only the one place that holds the filing rule; the\n   123\t  rebuilt request's archive id must equal the original's, or it is not sent (`resend_filing.py`, AGENTS §5). Never re-send the archive's copy whose\n   124\t  keys were sorted alphabetically.\n   125\t- **Articles**: development set = the 54 articles of `selection.json` (already read many times); held-out set = articles the extraction gold read and\n   126\t  the judgement gold uses that are not among those 54, drawn once before the test, with the list frozen and never changed afterwards. Both sets are\n   127\t  measured and reported apart.\n   128\t- **Control**: the unchanged requests re-sent 3 times the same day. No comparison with the archive (model drift).\n   129\t- **Each answer**: goes through the production code checks and filing first (including \"keep only the narrowest\" and \"no home means dropped\"), and is\n   130\t  counted after.\n   131\t- **What is counted** (`measure_filing.py`, with its matching rule fixed: items are matched on the distinct numbers and the proper nouns other than\n   132\t  NVIDIA in the source text; a source passage the system quotes that overlaps the gold's passage also counts as the same matter; \"optional\" counts as\n   133\t  optional; the citations in the body of the gold's answers are read):\n   134\t  - whether each fact that matters (one the gold uses to judge any question) reached the question that uses it, counted per fact, not per \"fact ×\n   135\t    question\";\n   136\t  - per question: the facts that matter that reached it and the new noise that appeared, counted by the decision rule below;\n   137\t  - the parking lot: how many facts the total-revenue question (v1, together with v22) takes in, and what they are;\n   138\t  - noise: how many of the sentences the standard does not keep are kept; per question, how noise is filed (links on facts the standard does not keep\n   139\t    or no question uses);\n   140\t  - how many core and supporting facts appear; the facts that matter that are dropped; how many questions each fact is filed under on average.\n   141\t  - Matching still has a small error (spot check: the new rule mismatches a few and misses a few); say so when reporting the numbers, and read the\n   142\t    ones that matter one by one.\n   143\t- **Decision rule** (rewritten on 2026-11-04 with Alex's agreement, D360. The original draft required \"every draw above the highest of the control's\n   144\t  three\" and the like, which the control itself fails when measured again, so it cannot tell good from bad. Script `decide.py`; written down and frozen\n   145\t  before each test; each draw's numbers and the totals are all reported):\n   146\t  - **Totals, with luck ruled out first**: each article's 6 answers from the new and the old arm are randomly reassigned between the two arms 4,000\n   147\t    times (with a fixed random seed); the new rule's difference counts as a real change only when fewer than 5% of the reassignments reach it.\n   148\t    - Development set: facts that matter reaching the question that uses them must rise; the count under the total-revenue questions (v1, v22) must\n   149\t      fall; sentences the standard does not keep must not rise; core facts must not fall.\n   150\t    - Held-out set: facts that matter reaching the question that uses them must not fall; sentences the standard does not keep must not rise; core\n   151\t      facts must not fall.\n   152\t  - **Per question, counted by code against the gold** (development and held-out sets together):\n   153\t    - A fact that matters which reaches the question at least two fewer times under the new rule than under the control counts as \"one fewer\" for that\n   154\t      question; in under the control all three times and under the new rule none of the three, it counts as \"lost outright\". A move to another question\n   155\t      that the gold also uses it for and that is no broader than the original (one home per fact, D207) does not count as fewer; a move to a broader\n   156\t      question counts. \"One more\" in the opposite direction offsets it.\n   157\t    - A noise sentence (not kept by the standard, or kept but used by no question) that the new rule files under the question at least twice while the\n   158\t      control never does counts as \"new noise\"; filed all three times, it counts as \"outright new noise\". The opposite direction offsets it.\n   159\t    - Any question with a net 3 fewer, a net 2 lost outright, a net 5 more new noise, or a net 3 more outright new noise fails. These thresholds are the\n   160\t      smallest values that keep \"misjudged when new and old are exactly the same\" within about one in ten; at the test, first compute the\n   161\t      misjudgement rate from the reassignments; if it is above one in ten, raise the threshold most prone to misjudgement by one step, then compare with\n   162\t      what was measured.\n   163\t  - **Smaller changes** (a question 2 fewer, two or three more noise) cannot be told from luck in three measurements: all are listed, given to a reader\n   164\t    who did not write the rule to read against the raw answers, and written into the report; they do not decide pass or fail.\n   165\t  Only when everything passes does it become a candidate; a candidate runs end to end three times on the 14-day window (extraction re-sent, matching and\n   166\t  judging run as usual, scored on the frozen judgement ruler), and that step needs Alex's word.\n   167\t\n   168\t## 7. Process (no drift)\n   169\t\n   170\t- **One recommendation and one test at a time** (AGENTS §7.3, Alex: \"就测试一种，一次\"): write the design first and put it through rounds of review (own\n   171\t  checks, Codex read-only, a reader who did not write it reading the raw records) until a round finds nothing serious; write down and freeze the\n   172\t  predictions and stop conditions; test once; if it does not match, stop and take it to Alex (D355: \"如果有什么不符合hypothesis 的地方你需要终止然后和我说我们重新评估\").\n   173\t- **A review's report is a lead**: each finding counts only once it is checked against the records (AGENTS §1).\n   174\t- **A prompt audit before any prompt change** (`.claude/skills/prompt-audit/SKILL.md`): check the whole request against the standard sentence by\n   175\t  sentence, in both directions.\n   176\t- **Every step goes into `log.md`**: what was done, what came of it, the files; mistakes and overturned results stay in it too.\n   177\t- **Do not repeat what was ruled out** (§3).\n   178\t\n   179\t## 8. Steps and where we are\n   180\t\n   181\t| # | Step | Output | Status |\n   182\t|---|---|---|---|\n   183\t| 1 | Design: the classes of indirect relation (a closed set + examples from other industries), how to file when a fact measures both sides, deleting the fallback; checked against the standard sentence by sentence; length before and after | `docs/research/2026-11-04-filing-end-to-end/design-links.md` | Done (draft 3, v74l / poc-v115) |\n   184\t| 2 | Checks at no cost: a sample answer written by the new rule goes through every code check; the new rule's text goes through the prompt audit | Same as above | Done |\n   185\t| 3 | Review rounds (Codex, independent readers, own), until nothing serious remains | `review-links-*.md` | Done (Codex 7 rounds, independent readers 3 times; round 7 found nothing serious) |\n   186\t| 4 | Draw and freeze the held-out set; write down and freeze this test's predictions and decision rule | `heldout.json`, `predictions-links.md` | Done (sha256 recorded for both) |\n   187\t| 5 | Test once: development set + held-out set, the new rule and the same-day control 3 times each | `answers-links.json`, `log.md` | Done (2026-11-04): failed: facts that matter reaching the right question, development set +7 (p 0.26); every other condition passed |\n   188\t| 6 | If passed: to Alex, to decide whether to run three 14-day end-to-end draws; if failed: stop, to Alex | `design-links.md` §8 | Stopped, to Alex |\n   189\t| 7 | (D361, Alex: \"按照你的提以来，但是你一定是自己反思多轮之后才给出的行动\") Measure the ceiling first: hand the source passages the gold cites straight to the frozen judgement; the custom-chip, operating-margin and capital-spending questions judged 3 times each at the window end, the custom-chip question as it was 3 times as the control; the fallback sentence is not deleted for now | `reflection-v74l.md`, `oracle-design.md` | Done: operating margin grounded 3/3, capital spending 2/3, custom chips 0/3 (the gap is in interpretation); the next step waits for Alex |\n   190\t| 8 | (D362, Alex: \"你继续啊不要scope drift and strongly follow evidence of your discovery and learning\") Two small changes where facts are not delivered, one at a time: ① when a quote is rewritten, code matches it back to the source sentence (changing nothing the model sees; verified by replaying the archive); ② a figure question's description states which figures it is derived from by definition (first sent 3 times each on the article that motivated it) | `delivery-fixes.md` | Stopped: an independent check overturned the first change's code (it would take in wrong figures); the second failed its trial send (1/3). The root: the signed standard's §5.3 does not keep two key facts, treating them as \"a restatement of known facts\" (proposal 6, awaiting approval); to Alex |\n   191\t| 9 | **Historical; superseded by D370.** (D363, Alex: \"乙\") At coverage start, record each question's starting figure once from the company's latest earnings report; the daily path still takes no restatements: design, rounds of self-checks and independent checks, validated first on the archive in the simplest way | `baseline-design.md` | Validated on two questions: the frozen extraction does not read the report's tables (0/3); with each question reading the report once on its own, the operating-margin question read its starting figure 2/3 and the capital-spending question 3/3, and the judgement then judged right (2/3, 3/3), about 2.5 cents. All 22 questions tested once (2026-11-05, Alex: \"你充分验证\"; files chosen by code, a same-day control, about $0.43): v10 and v12 judged right 9/9 each; no question already judged right turned wrong; but 5 of the 53 figures read were other quantities (the Vera CPU question and the Blackwell and Rubin question took a larger or neighbouring revenue line), so it failed condition 3, written beforehand. The model itself pointed to the reading instructions' sentence \"write the lines it is computed from, or the line the report uses for it\"; stopped, to Alex. Alex: \"改\"; only this sentence was changed (\"write only the lines it is computed from, or the same quantity under another name; never a broader line or a line next to it\"), tried on 4 questions first, then tested on all 22 (about $0.36): all three conditions passed: v10 and v12 9/9 each, no question judged right turned wrong, and all 46 figures were the questions' own quantities. Checked after an independent reader's challenge: the new sentence made the reading miss the capital-spending line in 3 of 12 reads, and it had been tested only on the one May report; the fourth version (a line-by-line re-check after writing) removed too much and failed; after a census of every failure trace (Alex: \"我需要你深度看完所有失败trace给我准信\"), the third version with the repaired quote check was chosen (D365): 0 wrong figures on the held-out August report; built into the production system (poc-v116; the $0 checks and the tests all pass). The full acceptance, three 14-day draws (Alex: \"跑验收\"; a same-day control; about $4.79): all passed: operating margin and capital spending judged right 3/3 each (the control never judged them), 53 against 45 in total (of 57); for the China-share question, the only one two fewer, both readers found the starting figures did not lead it astray. Alex: \"都做\": poc-v116 became the default (D366); the code was committed and a PR opened |\n   192\t\n   193\t## 9. Alex's own words on this matter (in time order)\n   194\t\n   195\t- \"你这个单独开一个专题出来解决 … 是promot，是系统结构设计还是到底哪里有问题挂错一堆东西，机制是不是有问题（一定要挂吗？有没有可能是杂音\" (2026-11-03)\n   196\t- \"So if it's a customer problem or other companies that you think is relating NVIDIA and it's the wrong evidence, then what is the standard for it? … from a hedge fund analyst standpoint, are these even relevant? Do you think your ruler is wrong in this aspect?\" (2026-11-04)\n   197\t- \"你确定下你接下来要改进的量化目标，以及你要看到端到端我们的管线是要做什么的，不要乱硬优化，要看到目标\" (2026-11-04)\n   198\t- \"就测试一种，一次 … 能不能自己有个超级自信的定论和证据再给我提建议\" (2026-11-04)\n   199\t- \"这些只是类型题，你需要抽象拔高我们现在的核心问题和卡点。这个很重要这样改下去才是对的\" (2026-11-04)\n   200\t- \"你把这个写好成下一阶段核心PRD然后不要drift, 把所有需要的context 全部充分记录，每次开始前都需要revisit, 然后开始做\" (2026-11-04)\n   201\t- \"go\" (2026-11-04, on the proposed amendments to §5.2 and §6 and on this test)\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_10","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"rg -n '7\\\\.3|Then converge|Code owns identity|Never grow|A failed arm|a measurement that removes|\"'^## 9|D73[34]|2026-11-22-number-answers'\"' AGENTS.md docs/CURRENT_STATE.md\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_10","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"rg -n '7\\\\.3|Then converge|Code owns identity|Never grow|A failed arm|a measurement that removes|\"'^## 9|D73[34]|2026-11-22-number-answers'\"' AGENTS.md docs/CURRENT_STATE.md\"","aggregated_output":"AGENTS.md:39:  work, as one recommendation and one test (§7.3). Settle wording, placement and where a rule is written yourself, and say so in the\nAGENTS.md:81:Then converge or stop:\nAGENTS.md:111:- **Code owns identity, never importance.** Before building a closed list, enum or gate, write which decision it takes from the model\nAGENTS.md:220:- **Never grow a prompt to fix quality.** Read the whole request (system, schema, payload) as the model reads it, and list every\nAGENTS.md:326:### 7.3 Iterating\nAGENTS.md:370:- **A failed arm is diagnosed before the next one.** When an arm fixes its class and regresses another, write the root cause first: the\nAGENTS.md:419:## 9. Workspace and operations\nAGENTS.md:428:  allows (§7.3) starts only on his explicit word (Alex, 2026-10-27: \"我不是要你看下step 1抽取到底行不行？\").\ndocs/CURRENT_STATE.md:448:**The one next task: Alex chooses between a bounded repair of judging-v13 and closing it (D733).**\ndocs/CURRENT_STATE.md:460:- **Record:** `research/2026-11-22-number-answers/PAID-GATE-RESULT.md`. Nothing has been changed or sent since.\ndocs/CURRENT_STATE.md:467:- Council round 45/45b (`research/2026-11-22-number-answers/RESULT.md`): the numbers fail because related questions have no shared,\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_11","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"for file in codex-r48 claude-r48 codex-r49 claude-r49; do rg -n '\"'^'\"' docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/\"'$file.md; done'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_11","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"for file in codex-r48 claude-r48 codex-r49 claude-r49; do rg -n '\"'^'\"' docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/\"'$file.md; done'","aggregated_output":"1:**The paid gate hit STOP. Judging-v13 needs one blind, zero-cost test of its answer format before another paid run.** Every reply wrote a forecast in words where the checker expected a figure ID. The request never said that field needed an ID. I also found checks that rejected correct calculations, including one that turned $411 billion × 92.5% into $38,017.5 billion. The run establishes that this version could publish nothing. It leaves open whether judging related questions together improves the forecasts. [Paid gate, §4](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/PAID-GATE.md:250)\n2:\n3:## 1. Census\n4:\n5:I matched all 72 finished entries to their requests and reproduced every recorded refusal in memory. Each journal also contained 36 `started` markers. The first attempts cost $0.155358 and the retries cost $0.212361, for **$0.367719 total**. All 36 first attempts and all 36 retries were refused. That gives the joint arm **0 of 9 usable pairs** against the required 8 of 9. [First attempts](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13-paid-gate-2026-11-22/answers.jsonl) · [Retries](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13-paid-gate-2026-11-22/retries.jsonl)\n6:\n7:Each check cell shows **refusal messages / replies affected**. A reply can have several messages.\n8:\n9:| Attempt | Arm | Replies | C7 structure | C1 source | C2 arithmetic or bounds | C8 compatible inputs | Stopped at first structure check |\n10:|---|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|\n11:| First | Joint | 9 | 39 / 9 | 0 | 0 | 0 | 9 |\n12:| First | Separate | 18 | 47 / 18 | 8 / 1 | 0 | 0 | 17 |\n13:| First | Singletons | 9 | 13 / 9 | 3 / 1 | 0 | 0 | 8 |\n14:| Retry | Joint | 9 | 40 / 9 | 0 | 0 | 0 | 9 |\n15:| Retry | Separate | 18 | 41 / 18 | 25 / 4 | 6 / 3 | 14 / 4 | 14 |\n16:| Retry | Singletons | 9 | 12 / 9 | 7 / 3 | 1 / 1 | 2 / 2 | 6 |\n17:\n18:- **C7, first → retry.** Undefined figure references 51 → 45, point/range violations 28 → 24, wrong operand counts 9 → 9, invalid signs 9 → 7, missing outcome links 2 → 7, cycles 0 → 1. All 52 point/range violations supplied both a point and a range. The checker stopped 63 replies before checking source figures. It also skipped the rung and reference checks when it could not find the outcome figure. That explains why only nine missing-outcome messages were logged even though all 72 replies had that problem. [Structure and early return](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/calc_check.py:302) · [Outcome, rung and reference checks](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/calc_check.py:468)\n19:- **Other classes.** C1 first → retry was 8 → 10 unrecognized evidence references, 2 → 6 expression mismatches, and 1 → 16 value/unit/component/bound mismatches. The retries exposed five C2 arithmetic and two bound refusals, plus ten C8 bridge, three period, two share and one overlap refusal. C3–C6 and C9–C10 had zero recorded messages. Among singletons, equity commitments produced all three first-attempt C1 messages. On retry, equity commitments produced five C1, one C2 and one C8. Vera CPU produced two C1 and one C8. Hyperscaler spending stopped at C7 in all six replies. [First attempts](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13-paid-gate-2026-11-22/answers.jsonl) · [Retries](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13-paid-gate-2026-11-22/retries.jsonl)\n20:\n21:I counted nonempty values at the named fields against the exact IDs available to that reply. The table counts **occurrences**, so a reply with two forecasts can contribute two outcome entries.\n22:\n23:| Field containing prose | First joint / separate / single | First total | Retry joint / separate / single | Retry total |\n24:|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|\n25:| `figures[].evidence` | 67 / 96 / 12 | 175 | 35 / 88 / 10 | 133 |\n26:| `figures[].bridge` | 25 / 18 / 6 | 49 | 23 / 17 / 4 | 44 |\n27:| `answers[].outcome` | 18 / 18 / 9 | 45 | 18 / 18 / 9 | 45 |\n28:| `answers[].reference.figure` | 16 / 15 / 8 | 39 | 12 / 13 / 7 | 32 |\n29:| `figures[].converts[]` | 0 / 0 / 2 | 2 | 0 / 0 / 0 | 0 |\n30:| `figures[].disputed` | 0 / 0 / 1 | 1 | 0 / 0 / 2 | 2 |\n31:| `figures[].inputs[]` | 0 / 0 / 0 | 0 | 0 / 1 / 0 | 1 |\n32:\n33:**Corrections to Claude’s counts.** The first-attempt counts of 49 bridges, 45 outcomes, 39 references and two `converts` entries match. The 175 evidence fields hold prose, but only **139** are source figures where C1 requires a passage ID. The retry split is **95 source figures out of 133**. The one `adjusts.figure` entry is `e2652`, a real passage ID in a field that needs a figure ID. The same value also appears in `inputs[]` on that figure. Claude missed the `disputed` prose in the equity-commitment first attempt. Two retry `converts[]` entries likewise use passage IDs where operand IDs are expected. Finally, the four units Claude lists are the source parser’s units. The checker also uses `units` for per-unit calculations, and it has no general unit check for estimates. [First-attempt wrong ID](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13-paid-gate-2026-11-22/answers.jsonl:6) · [First-attempt overlap prose](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13-paid-gate-2026-11-22/answers.jsonl:68) · [Source units](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/calc_check.py:45) · [Per-unit calculation](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/calc_check.py:665)\n34:\n35:## 2. What the refusals mean\n36:\n37:**A** and **R** refer to line numbers in the first-attempt and retry journals. I read the figures and their inputs against the passages in the actual request. “Exposed on replay” means an earlier refusal hid the check in the original run. The verdict in each row applies to that refusal. [First attempts](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13-paid-gate-2026-11-22/answers.jsonl) · [Retries](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13-paid-gate-2026-11-22/retries.jsonl) · [Requests as sent](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/paid-plan.json)\n38:\n39:| Reply | Figure and evidence | Rule and finding |\n40:|---|---|---|\n41:| **A2, joint** | Q3 guidance **$108B, range $105.84–110.16B**. `e2652` says “$108.0 billion, plus or minus 2%.” Both the midpoint and endpoints are correct. | **(b) Unstated format.** C7 says “either a value or a complete range.” The schema accepts all three numbers together and gives no instruction to split the midpoint and range. [C7](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/calc_check.py:319) |\n42:| **A2, joint** | Q4 estimate **$112.3B**, from a reconstructed $68.1B base and `e5162`’s 65% growth. `bridge` explains that conversion in a sentence. | **(b) Unstated format.** C7 says the sentence “is not defined.” The schema calls `bridge` “an estimate stating the conversion” and never says to put the ID of a separate estimate there. [Sent schema](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/paid-plan.json:237) · [C7](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/calc_check.py:310) |\n43:| **A2, joint** | Outcome **“$398.1 billion”** corresponds to the reply’s own annual figure. Its quarterly inputs are $81.62B, $96.2B, $108B and $112.3B, based on `e111`, `e2649`, `e2652` and the Q4 estimate above. | **(b) Unstated format, exposed on replay.** C7 says the outcome “is not a figure.” The schema gives `outcome` as a nullable string with no description. [Sent schema](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/paid-plan.json:360) · [C7](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/calc_check.py:488) |\n44:| **A6, separate** | The reference reads **“dc_fy27_reference: Morningstar estimates $361 billion”**. The named source figure exists and `e2295` states $361B for FY2027 Data Center revenue. | **(b) Unstated format, masked in the original.** C5 would say “the reference names no figure” because the lookup uses the entire string. The schema describes the reference as “an external figure” without telling the writer to return only its ID. [Sent schema](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/paid-plan.json:380) · [C5](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/calc_check.py:779) |\n45:| **A6, separate** | The **$113.6B** Q3 estimate uses the outside forecast in `e5056` and the $108B guidance in `e2652`. It puts `e2652` in both `inputs` and `adjusts.figure`. | **(b) Unstated format.** C7 reports twice that `e2652` “is not defined.” It is defined as evidence. The schema fails to distinguish a source passage ID from a figure ID in those fields. [C7](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/calc_check.py:310) |\n46:| **A12, separate** | Q4 residual **$125.2B** equals $411B − $81.6B − $96.2B − $108B, from `e4097`, `e238`, `e2497` and `e2499`. The model used `difference` with four inputs and signs. | **(b) Unstated operation rules.** C7 says “difference takes 2 operands, not 4” and limits `signs` to “a sum or roll_forward.” The schema lists the operation names without their operand or sign rules. This verdict covers the arithmetic representation. Treating the full-year consensus residual as a forecast is a separate judgement. [C7](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/calc_check.py:327) |\n47:| **A44, equity singleton** | **`25 USD billions`**, cited to `e2620`, which reports $25B in commitments as of July 26. | **(b) Unstated unit spelling.** C1 says the value “does not equal ‘$25 billion’ as component value.” It compares the unit string to `USD bn`. The amount agrees and `unit` was a free string. [C1](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/calc_check.py:85) |\n48:| **A2, joint** | **65% FY2027 Q4 growth**. The reply copied “65% growth in Q4” directly from `e5162`. | **(b) Unstated span boundary, exposed on replay.** C1 says the phrase “is not a complete numeric expression” because it extracts `65%` alone. With the passage ID and `%` set, changing only the expression to `65%` cleared C1. The request did not explain where wording around a number must end. [C1](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/calc_check.py:78) |\n49:| **A24, Vera CPU singleton** | **$42B**, with a **$40–50B** forecast range. Its input was the threshold of more than $40B, calculated from the $20B base and “more than double” in `e3660` and `e4856`. | **(b) Unstated format.** C7 says “either a value or a complete range.” Reporting a central estimate alongside a range is reasonable under the schema as sent. Whether $42B is a supported forecast still needs a reader. [C7](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/calc_check.py:319) |\n50:| **A2, joint** | First-half Data Center revenue **$164.25B**, from Q1 $75.25B (`e109`) plus Q2 $89B (`e2650`). | **(c) Check too narrow, exposed on replay.** C8 requires “one quantity over disjoint periods forming FY2027 first half.” The periods do that. `_quarters` recognizes `H1` and cannot read `first half`. [Period parser](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/calc_check.py:285) · [C8](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/calc_check.py:411) |\n51:| **A4, separate** | FY2027 Q4 **$112.2B** from $68B FY2026 Q4 actual (`e2452`) × 1.65, using the stated 65% year-over-year forecast (`e5162`). | **(c) Check too narrow, exposed on replay.** C8 reports “no bridge naming those operands.” It gives `apply_growth` an exception tied to a declared `same_base` family relation. The source already states this particular year-over-year link. Carrying a Q3 sequential rate into Q4 would be a different case and would still need an assumption. [C8](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/calc_check.py:434) |\n52:| **A8, joint** | Data Center reference proxy **$380.175B**, from $411B FY2027 company consensus (`e4097`) × the reported 92.5% Q2 mix (`e3216`). | **(c) Wrong arithmetic check, exposed on replay.** C2 says `product` “gives 38017.50.” It multiplies by 92.5 as a plain number, while `share` divides by 100. The arithmetic is correct. Applying a Q2 mix to a full-year reference remains an assumption. [Arithmetic](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/calc_check.py:125) · [C2](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/calc_check.py:371) |\n53:| **R12, separate** | **`> $300B`** for Data Center revenue. `e2115` says “well over $300 billion.” The reply kept both the words and the lower bound. | **(c) Check too narrow.** C1 says the quoted phrase “is not a complete numeric expression.” Its parser finds `over $300 billion` by starting one word later. It penalizes the reply for preserving the full qualifier. [Parser](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/calc_check.py:12) · [C1](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/calc_check.py:78) |\n54:| **R66, separate** | Annual Data Center calculation **$375.788B**, from the first six months’ $164B (`e5360`) plus $99.9B and $111.888B for Q3 and Q4. The later quarters use the guidance and mix in `e2715`, `e3453` and `e4190`. | **(c) Check too narrow.** C8 requires “disjoint periods forming FY2027.” On the stored reply, changing only `FY2027 first six months` to `FY2027 H1` removed exactly this refusal. The calculation’s other refusals remained. [C8](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/calc_check.py:411) |\n55:| **R44, equity singleton** | **$46.5B** = $25B + $18B + $3.5B. The reply puts a sentence about MediaTek’s overlap in `disputed`. `e2620` gives the July balance. `e2889` says $18B is on top of **$47.9B already held**, which does not establish that it is on top of the **$25B already committed**. | **(b) Unstated format for the recorded refusal.** C8 says “overlap_unknown must name a disputed operand.” The schema does not say to provide that operand’s ID. **(a) Separate judgement gap.** Changing the prose to the MediaTek figure ID would leave the addition of $25B and $18B resting on an undeclared assumption. [C8](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/calc_check.py:418) |\n56:| **R60, separate** | The **15%** Q4 growth estimate rests on the **$124.2B** company and **$114.885B** Data Center forecasts, both computed using that same 15%. `e3222` supports reacceleration qualitatively and gives no rate. | **(a) Model error.** C7 says “the figure graph has a cycle.” The forecast figures cannot serve as independent support for the rate that produces them. Renaming fields leaves the circular reasoning intact. [C7](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/calc_check.py:341) |\n57:| **R22, hyperscaler singleton** | **$1.05 trillion** in rung 3, based on a judgemental discount to the $1.3T projection and the $1.08T consensus (`e4756`, `e1926`). | **(c) Wrong unit comparison, exposed on replay.** After resolving the outcome link and setting aside the prose bridge on a copy, C6 says the $900–1,100B band “contains no part” of `1.05`. Converting the dollar figures to billions cleared the remaining checks. The reply itself says neither scope adjustment was quantified. A code pass still leaves the reader to decide whether the adjustment is supported. [C6](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/calc_check.py:495) |\n58:\n59:One more source boundary matters for the proposed fix. R44’s **US$3.5B** appears in the evidence summary for `e3558`, but the attached quote only describes the convertible bonds. `e3474`, also shown, has the amount in its quote. The model sees both summaries and quotes. C1 checks only the quote attached to the selected ID. The request needs to say which text can support a source figure. A match on the amount alone cannot settle that. [Request evidence and quote handling](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/paid_gate.py:55) · [C1](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/calc_check.py:78)\n60:\n61:I reproduced Claude’s diagnostic **0 publishes** and **9 replies with no C7** on the first attempts. The retries give the same counts. The phrase “past the structural layer” needs a correction. **25 of 36** in each journal passed the *early* structural check after the script’s edits. Later outcome-link failures reduced that to nine without any C7. The script also guesses links from matching amounts, deletes prose bridges and drops valid range endpoints when a midpoint is present. Its default options omit the `prose` pass used to reproduce these counts. These are useful probes for finding hidden checks. They do not establish how many replies a repaired request would get right, or that most of the 72 replies had sound forecasts. [Diagnostic script](/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-lchingen-hivemind/f1ea972d-85a1-4f27-8010-cf0b60054109/scratchpad/normalise_diag.py:12)\n62:\n63:## 3. Why the zero-cost gate missed it\n64:\n65:**The author of the passing answer already knew the checker’s rules.** The fixtures hand in the expected IDs, units and period spellings. The paid rehearsal’s clean answer goes further and uses an estimate inside a preselected rung, with no source calculation and an unavailable reference. That proves the harness can carry a conforming answer and that planted defects can trigger checks. It leaves unanswered whether someone reading the exact request could write a conforming answer without that inside knowledge. [Registered controls](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/DESIGN.md:152) · [Clean rehearsal fixture](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/paid_rehearsal.py:42)\n66:\n67:The missed step was a **blind write-and-check using the exact outgoing request**. Give an independent writer only the system text, user text and schema. Have them encode real forecasts and then run their first outputs through the actual harness. For each refusal, ask the writer to point to where the request stated the rule. Read the check back in the other direction for correct answers it cannot represent. An evidence ID enum would have prevented the most common source mistake. The request already uses one for citations. [AGENTS §6](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/AGENTS.md:231) · [AGENTS §7.3](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/AGENTS.md:370) · [Sent schema](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/paid-plan.json:120)\n68:\n69:## 4. One recommendation and one test\n70:\n71:**Recommendation.** Keep the STOP and ask Alex to open one bounded repair of the judging-v13 request and checker. Put each field’s rule next to the field. Use enums for known IDs, units and choices where the schema can enforce them. Fix the demonstrated arithmetic, period and source-boundary failures while preserving checks for genuine scope changes, unsupported assumptions and circular calculations. Code already extracts numeric expressions for verification. Sending a complete catalog of those expressions to the model could remove some copying, but would also make today’s parser omissions into missing evidence. It would leave the universal outcome-link failure untouched. Closing v13 now would save further work, but the result has not answered D725’s question about shared calculations. One bounded test can determine whether there is a workable way to express those calculations at all. [D725](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/DECISIONS-log.md:10957) · [D690](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/DECISIONS-log.md:10360)\n72:\n73:**Test.** Run one blind conformance test at $0 on fresh examples covering a joint pair, its two separate replies, and all three singleton calculation shapes. Before the test, register correct examples and deliberately broken counterparts, including a wrong number, a wrong period or scope, an unresolved overlap and a circular calculation. Run the independent writer’s first replies through the real harness without hand edits. **Stop if any correct form is refused, any planted defect gets through its intended check, or the reader still needs an unwritten rule to encode a correct answer.** Use the 72 stored replies to check individual fixes where the intended figure and source are identifiable. A guessed link or rewritten answer earns no credit. A clean result earns a request to rerun the same 36-call comparison under a fresh decision, with the original pass lines. The current gate’s STOP stands. [Registered reading and stop rules](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/PAID-GATE.md:250)\n1:# Round 48, Claude's own reflection (written before reading Codex's report)\n2:\n3:## The result as registered\n4:\n5:- 36 scored first attempts, 36 diagnostic retries, $0.3677 spent of $0.50 (ledger: 72 attempts, no refusal by the ledger, not stopped).\n6:- All 72 answers were refused by `calc_check.py`; nothing was published. Every attempt finished (status `answered`), so the result is\n7:  complete, not INCOMPLETE.\n8:- By D725's lines: the joint arm is usable in 0 of 9 pairs. **STOP.**\n9:- The blind reading cannot change this. The registered reader brief says a card with no answer cannot be usable, and every card is\n10:  \"no answer\". The readers are not run; the packets can still be rendered and sealed at no cost if the record needs them.\n11:\n12:## What the result measures\n13:\n14:It does not measure the question the gate was built for (does judging a family in one call give better numbers than judging each\n15:member alone?). It measures whether the model can fill v13's answer format as the request states it. It cannot.\n16:\n17:## The mechanism (traced on stored answers, both journals)\n18:\n19:1. **The format's rules live in the check, not in the request.** The sent schema has 7 descriptions (`what`, `basis`, `kind`,\n20:   `expression`, `relation`, `bridge`, `reference`). These fields have none: `value`, `low`, `high`, `bound`, `unit`, `evidence`,\n21:   `component`, `inputs`, `operation`, `signs`, `disputed`, `basis_text`, `rests_on`, `converts`, `scenario`, and `outcome` and `rung` on\n22:   the answers. The check enforces closed conventions on them that the request never states:\n23:   - `evidence` is a passage id, while the model wrote the sentence (175 figures in the first attempts);\n24:   - `outcome` and `reference.figure` are figure ids, while the model wrote \"$398.1 billion\" and sentences (45 and 39);\n25:   - `bridge` is a figure id, while its own description reads as prose (\"an estimate stating the conversion …\"), and the model\n26:     wrote prose (49);\n27:   - `unit` must be exactly `USD bn`, `%`, `x` or `USD`; the model wrote `USD billions`, `$ billion`, `percent` and `fraction`;\n28:   - a figure has a value or a range, never both; a guidance \"$108.0 billion, plus or minus 2%\" was read as value, low, high,\n29:     bound `approx` and component `midpoint`;\n30:   - a period is labelled `FY2027 H1`, not \"FY2027 first half\" or \"first six months\";\n31:   - a percentage is applied with `share` (which divides by 100), not `product`;\n32:   - `range_from_relative` takes the midpoint and the relative figure as two operands;\n33:   - `signs` exists only on `sum` and `roll_forward`, and a four-term difference is a `sum` with signs.\n34:2. **Even with these corrected mechanically on copies, nothing publishes.** 9 of 36 first attempts get past the structural layer, and\n35:   0 publish. The next layer includes:\n36:   - the expression copied with its words (\"65% growth in Q4\", where the extractor yields \"65%\");\n37:   - the period vocabulary;\n38:   - `apply_growth` of last year's quarter by a year-over-year growth. The check accepts this without a bridge only when the family\n39:     has a `same_base` relation, and the bridge description's list of identities does not include growth;\n40:   - some arithmetic that follows from the conventions above.\n41:3. **One wrong answer anywhere refuses the whole answer.** An answer carries about 20 figures, and each figure must pass several\n42:   rules. A long conjunction fails by chance even when each part is usually right (AGENTS §7.1, \"a conjunction over many items fails\n43:   by chance\"). The retries carried every reason, and they fixed nothing: 36 of 36 were refused again, mostly on the same classes (prose where an id\n44:   is expected in 20, value and range in 22).\n45:\n46:## My process error\n47:\n48:- AGENTS §6: \"Check before the send, at no cost. Write one answer that follows the prompt and run every code check on it.\"\n49:  - I wrote the $0 gate's answer knowing the check's conventions. It proved that the check accepts a conforming answer, not that\n50:    the request teaches one.\n51:  - The step that would have caught this at no cost: an answer written by a reader who sees only the request, run through the\n52:    checks.\n53:- REQUEST-AUDIT.md says \"field definitions moved to the schema\". Only 7 descriptions reached the sent schema, and I did not check the\n54:  sent bytes against that sentence.\n55:- The eight council rounds (47 to 47h) reviewed the harness: the journals, the keys, the packets and the verdict. None reviewed the\n56:  request as the model reads it against the check.\n57:- The \"rests_on prose\" diagnosis I gave Alex mid-run was wrong in its field. The prose sat in `bridge`, `evidence`, `outcome` and\n58:  `reference`, not in `rests_on`. I correct it in the report.\n59:\n60:## The way forward (my lean, to be challenged)\n61:\n62:- The gate as registered says STOP. It is recorded as written (D680).\n63:- What the result can still decide: whether a v13-style calculation can be filled at all. That decides whether the number-answer\n64:  work continues on this path.\n65:- Lean:\n66:  - **(ii) first:** move every decision code can make out of the model.\n67:    - Code extracts each numeric expression from the shown passages and lists it as a numbered source figure, with value, unit,\n68:      bound and period parsed by code. The model only references these by id. This removes the expression, evidence, unit and\n69:      value-or-range classes at their owner.\n70:    - `outcome`, `reference.figure`, `bridge`, `inputs` and `evidence` become enums of the ids the request holds where the strict\n71:      schema allows it.\n72:  - **(i):** every remaining field gets its rule in the schema beside it, including the operation and period vocabulary, and the\n73:    check's too-narrow rules are written down as decisions before any change.\n74:  - **The test, all at $0 before any send:**\n75:    - every stored answer is re-read under the changed contract, and no right answer may be refused;\n76:    - a blind reader who sees only the new request writes answers for three families, and they must pass;\n77:    - then the same 36-request gate, about $0.40, on Alex's word.\n78:  - **Stop condition:** if a blind reader's answers still fail on rules the request states, the format is too heavy for one call.\n79:    In that case close judging-v13 and keep poc-v118 (D690).\n80:- Not certain:\n81:  - (ii) is a new design piece (code builds the source figures), not a wording fix. It needs its own design tables (AGENTS §2)\n82:    before code.\n83:  - Whether Alex wants to spend more on this path is his call.\n1:**Round 49 finds serious issues. Revise the design before writing code.** I replayed the proposed growth rule in memory. It accepted a source’s **12% sequential growth** as **12% year-over-year growth** once the answer relabelled the rate. The repair would let the answer supply its own proof that the rate applies.\n2:\n3:I rebuilt the original plan byte for byte, checked the stored first answers and retries, and reran the existing gate at $0. All 87 baseline cases passed. The counterexamples below test the proposed rules in memory.\n4:\n5:## Blockers\n6:\n7:1. **§3.2 trusts the answer’s growth label.** I used the actual 12% sequential guidance in `e3943`, labelled it `FY2027 Q4 vs FY2026 Q4`, and applied it to a $100B prior-year base. The current check refused it. A literal implementation of §3.2 accepted $112B. The positive control has a related problem. `e5162`’s exact quote is “and 65% growth in Q4.” Its summary supplies *year over year*. Under the quote-only rule, that item alone cannot prove the comparison. The design needs to say where both periods come from for a source rate, how a computed rate gets its periods from its operands, and when an assumption is required. A2 needs the **falsely relabelled** rate as well as the honestly labelled rate applied to the wrong base. Replacing the existing `same_base` exception also broke G4 in my replay. Keeping that exception as written leaves a shortcut triggered by any `same_base` relation in the family. [Growth check](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/calc_check.py:438) · [12% source](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/paid-plan.json:8722) · [65% as shown](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/paid-plan.json:2387) · [G4](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/gate.py:261)\n8:\n9:2. **§3.4 verifies where the words occur and can still reverse their meaning.** In my replay, `not be over $300 billion` passed as `> $300B`. The contained match was `over $300 billion`. `-12% sequentially` also passed as positive `12%`. A qualifier can sit outside both spans. In `$300 billion or more`, the parser extracts an exact `$300 billion`, so both versions accept a point. “Exactly one extracted expression” can also admit a second quantity the parser misses, such as `$300 billion and 50 units`. Keep the positive controls for `well over` and the longer Q4 phrase. Register the sign, negation, trailing qualifier and second-quantity cases before settling what the check can certify. [Repair §3.4](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/REPAIR-DESIGN.md:61) · [Extractor and C1](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/calc_check.py:12)\n10:\n11:3. **The blind test currently supplies the wrong question for E, and S needs its year corrected.** `old-2/8287` exists and really is `v41`, but it asks whether the Fed will raise rates. `old-3/8287` asks about an investment. Vera CPU FY2028 is `v41` in `old-3`, where I found 20 alternatives to paid call 9166. S’s `old-3/9043` correctly asks about four hyperscalers, for **calendar 2026**. Reusing the paid S key would ask for **CY2027** in `Outcome keys`. The render path accepts a caller-supplied member ID and copies the archived heading without checking that they describe the same question. Register the archive, call ID, question, period and ladder together. [Blind inputs](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/REPAIR-DESIGN.md:104) · [Paid case declarations](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/paid_gate.py:39) · [Render path](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/render_v13.py:85)\n12:\n13:## Rules still missing from the request\n14:\n15:I walked every refusal in `calc_check.py`. The proposal covers unique figure IDs, the individual field enums, figure ID versus passage ID for the renamed fields, and the exact outcome key in substance. The existing request already prints all four parts of the outcome key. The following rules are missing, incomplete or stated differently from what code enforces. [Proposed schema descriptions](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/REPAIR-DESIGN.md:35) · [Existing outcome keys](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/render_v13.py:104)\n16:\n17:| Check | What the request still needs to say or encode |\n18:|---|---|\n19:| **C1 — source numbers** | A source must supply a shown passage ID and a matching value, unit, bound and component. State the component choices by form. A money range supplies separate `low` and `high` figures, a relative range supplies `midpoint` and `relative`, and a multiplier uses `multiplier` and keeps its qualifier. Ordinary money and percentages use `value`, and qualified figures have specific `bound` rules. The enum names the components without telling the writer when each works. [C1](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/calc_check.py:78) |\n20:| **C2 — arithmetic** | State the point-versus-range comparison, exact zero for cancelling terms, allowed rounding, bound direction and what to do with opposing bounds or an unevaluable calculation. Code allows a point at the computed midpoint for most operations, checks both ends for a supplied range and `range_from_relative`, and uses a 0.5% tolerance with floors of 0.05 for percentages and 0.1 otherwise. Bounds can flip on subtraction or division. Opposing directions require an estimate. [Tolerance](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/calc_check.py:280) · [Refusals](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/calc_check.py:364) |\n21:| **C3 — part and whole** | Render the actual `compare_periods`. The checker uses each member’s population, measure and scope with each listed period and scenario. It refuses duplicate reachable forecast figures for either side and checks the part against the whole when both exist. It compares corresponding ends for points and closed ranges. With an open bound it asks whether any pair of values can fit. The current general instruction that a part cannot exceed its whole leaves these rules out. [Comparison](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/calc_check.py:553) · [Bounds](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/calc_check.py:210) |\n22:| **C4 — shared figures and scenarios** | The exact shared key is all four `what` fields. When an outcome touches that key, every path to it must pass through a forecast figure, with exactly one such figure per represented scenario. A point calculation or estimate cannot borrow a shared forecast or an estimate from another scenario, including through intermediate figures. Part and whole must cover the same estimate-derived scenarios. `range` means an estimate with both ends that serves as an outcome and feeds no figure. The enum alone supplies none of these rules. [Scenario and range checks](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/calc_check.py:512) · [Shared paths](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/calc_check.py:572) |\n23:| **C5 — reference** | A forecast needs exactly one marked reference or a nonempty reason. The rule applies to the entire calculation behind the reference. It must contain at least one external source whose speaker is other than `system`. Every source at the bottom must be external or reported. Estimates, earlier forecasts and figures labelled `forecast` are barred anywhere in the calculation. A direct reported figure alone fails. The individual `basis` and `kind` enums do not encode these combinations. There is no general `basis × kind` cross-check elsewhere beyond the prior rule below. [Choice](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/calc_check.py:503) · [Reference graph](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/calc_check.py:779) |\n24:| **C6 — rung** | A rung is an integer from 1 through that member’s ladder length. Its band must **overlap** the recomputed outcome. It can contain just part of a range or bounded outcome. Lower band edges are included and upper edges excluded. If several bands overlap, the reasons should explain the choice, as the original design requires. “The band contains the outcome” reads as full containment. [C6](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/calc_check.py:495) · [Band edges](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/calc_check.py:200) · [Original design](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/DESIGN.md:75) |\n25:| **C7 — structure** | State that all figure links must resolve and none may form a cycle, including through `rests_on`, adjustments and bridges. State exactly one answer per member and the unresolved shape explicitly, including a nonempty reason and no rung. Specify `low ≤ high`, the actual operand limits (`sum` 1–99, `product` and `roll_forward` 2–99, other operations exactly 2), and that null `signs` means all additions. The existing user text covers one answer per member, and the member enum covers unknown member IDs. The repair still needs to define duplicate answer and marker handling. [Figure checks](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/calc_check.py:302) · [Member checks](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/calc_check.py:468) |\n26:| **C8 — compatible inputs** | Scope the fiscal-period spellings to time aggregation, including same quantity, same fiscal year and disjoint periods that exactly form the result. Name which `sum`/`difference` relations permit differing keys, and that `accounting_parts` needs a caller-declared scope split. The paid builder supplies no `scope_parts`. For `roll_forward`, specify the opening, flows and closing formats. For `overlap_unknown`, the disputed ID must be an actual operand. For `share`, specify one percentage and one whole, whole first. For a bridge, say which operand IDs must match exactly. The current comparison excludes `%` and `x` operands when finding differing keys, and it checks the bridge’s kind and IDs but does not require assumption text. The multiplier exception and per-unit identity need full operand rules. Per-unit `ratio` uses total then price and produces a figure whose measure is `units`. `product` uses a count and price for the same population and scope. The check accepts price in `USD` **or** `USD bn`. [Compatibility](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/calc_check.py:398) · [Per-unit identity](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/calc_check.py:665) · [Paid family data](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/paid_gate.py:63) |\n27:| **C9 and C10 — bases and earlier answers** | C9 requires a value-determining path to the earlier member’s published forecast or the matching scenario behind its range, with no bypass. A citation alone fails, a base that cancels fails, and multiple earlier scenarios must be covered exactly. Code tests dependence by changing the earlier midpoint by 10%. The schema says none of this. C10 and C7’s prior-pointer refusal are closed off in the current wire format by the `kind` and `basis` enums and the absence of `pointer`. Preserve the internal tests for exact member, day and value, comparison-only use, and the ban on using a prior as an outcome or input. [C9 and C10](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/calc_check.py:599) · [Prior pointer](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/calc_check.py:357) · [Wire schema](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/render_v13.py:30) |\n28:\n29:## Major findings\n30:\n31:- **The markers can represent the legitimate old links.** They can mark a range summary as an outcome, let several members share one figure, let a forecast use a computed reference, and represent an unresolved member with neither marker. Define what happens if an unresolved member is marked for a reference or given an unavailable reason. The old checker skipped references for unresolved answers. Also define repeated member IDs on one figure, competing figures, and the treatment of an invalid answer during diagnostic rendering. `from_answer` is a reasonable single place to translate the wire shape. It needs a defined result on marker errors and a path to each raw-answer reader. `Gate.attempt`, `summarise` and `card_lines` each read the raw shape independently. Both scoring and diagnostic packet rendering use `card_lines`. `strict()` is the reverse direction for the render fixture and currently drops fields with new names. [Marker design](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/REPAIR-DESIGN.md:19) · [Unresolved branch](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/calc_check.py:484) · [First answer](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/paid_gate.py:165) · [Merge input](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/paid_gate.py:266) · [Packet input](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/paid_gate.py:501) · [Fixture encoder](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/render_v13.py:116)\n32:\n33:- **The scenario enum changes what the analyst can express.** `DESIGN.md` allows named scenarios and gives low and high as examples. Two different downside scenarios can have separate assumptions and forecasts. Forcing both into `low` can cause a duplicate-forecast refusal or mix the two calculations. The internal gate even uses `alt` to exercise cross-scenario refusals. Preserve named scenarios, or explicitly account for the narrower contract and prove that it covers the cases this repair is meant to support. [Allowed scenario names](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/DESIGN.md:27) · [Alternate scenario fixture](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/gate.py:338)\n34:\n35:- **The other §3 changes need sharper controls.** **3.1** adds no acceptance path. A closed list applied to every `what.period` would nevertheless exclude working values such as `rest of FY2027` and `period not specified`. Keep the list local to the operations that parse it. **3.3** would reject a correct calculation I confirmed passes today, `12% × 2x = 24%`. `share` would yield `0.24` with the same operands. The defect is multiplying a whole by a percentage as if the percentage were a raw multiplier. **3.5** excludes the spelling `USD trillion`, but the rung check still accepts `1.05` with an allowed unit and compares the raw number with billion-dollar bands. The enum does not itself verify magnitude or compatibility with the ladder. [Period parser](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/calc_check.py:285) · [Working period fixtures](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/gate.py:128) · [Product and share](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/calc_check.py:125) · [Rung comparison](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/calc_check.py:495)\n36:\n37:- **A1 and A2 miss cases that could decide the result.** The stored four-term residual, `$411B − $81.6B − $96.2B − $108B`, still fails C8 as a signed `sum`. I confirmed that omitting the relation fails and `time_aggregation` fails because the annual period overlaps the quarters. `stated_in_addition` passes even though it misdescribes the calculation. For A1, also carry a range outcome with a valid overlapping rung, a shared figure, a computed external reference, an unresolved answer, a roll-forward that records overlap, a valid scope bridge, both per-unit directions and a same-base calculation through the new wire shape. For A2, plant value plus range, a missing range end, duplicate member answers, conflicting reference markers, an outcome key mismatch, a shared-figure bypass, a wrong scenario, a forecast or estimate inside a reference, and a missing or wrong bridge. Test the actual cross-period share assumption too. Correct multiplication does not establish that a Q2 mix applies to a full year. Assert the intended refusal so a schema error cannot mask the check under test. [A1/A2](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/REPAIR-DESIGN.md:115) · [Stored residual](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/codex-r48.md:46) · [Sum compatibility](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/calc_check.py:407)\n38:\n39:- **The six requests test fewer than six independent evidence sets.** The joint pair and both separate requests intentionally share the same evidence. Each reuses **228 of 266** passage IDs from its corresponding paid request and **230 of 266** exact quotes appear somewhere in the paid plan. O reuses **30 of 30**. S has **9 of 50** exact quotes anywhere in the paid plan, including **6 of 50** in its corresponding paid request. E has no valid overlap figure as specified. A genuine Vera candidate, `old-3/9071`, reuses **15 of 15**, while `old-3/8753` reuses **9 of 9** by ID. The high-overlap cases are useful controls for the repair. The test needs a correctly identified request with new decisive evidence to claim it generalizes. Give each writer a fresh context per request. As written, each writer sees the entire batch and can carry its joint answer into the separate requests. [Input list and session setup](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/REPAIR-DESIGN.md:86) · [Evidence union](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/paid_gate.py:95)\n40:\n41:- **Part B can pass without demonstrating that a writer completed a usable calculation.** I ran an all-unresolved pair through the current checks. It returned zero errors and zero outcomes. Conversely, the design can call even **0 of 12** a clean conformance result if every refusal is attributed to the writer. The **6 of 12** warning has no stated basis and can hide a failure on every joint request. Register the forms each case can genuinely exercise, report supported forecasts, justified unresolved answers and refusals separately, and require successful coverage of the joint and singleton forms before calling the test clean. Have independent readers who did not write the design or the answers record two separate facts for each refusal: whether the exact outgoing request states the condition, and whether the answer is correct against the shown passages and governing design. Both problems can occur together. Resolve disagreements before declaring a pass. Classify truncation, session failure and checker exceptions separately from writer mistakes. Stronger writers can demonstrate that they can follow a single outgoing request. At $0, replay the observed Luna mistake patterns from both journals against the new schema and check that each is prevented or gets a clear reason, then compare strong writers on the old and repaired requests in separate contexts. Actual Luna compliance remains unmeasured. [Classification and stop rules](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/REPAIR-DESIGN.md:139) · [Unresolved branch](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/calc_check.py:479) · [Stored failure census](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/PAID-GATE-RESULT.md:25)\n42:\n43:## Build and record\n44:\n45:- **The rehearsal must read the new plan, and the old run must keep its reader.** The rehearsal loads `paid-plan.json` at import and validates outgoing bytes against that object. Updating stubs alone still rehearses the old schema. The equivalent repair run should consume a separately pinned plan and compare it with the old plan by case, evidence, questions, family data and intended request changes. The old journals store the raw old shape. Replacing `strip` everywhere would reinterpret those entries during packet rendering and recovery. Recovery also rewrites `results.json`. Bind decoding and checking to the plan version and verify historical reproduction on a copy. I confirmed the current plan hash matches its pin, the run’s plan has identical data, and the current builder recreates the exact original plan bytes. [Rehearsal plan](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/paid_rehearsal.py:18) · [Byte comparison](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/paid_rehearsal.py:120) · [Recovery and send pin](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/paid_gate.py:365)\n46:\n47:- **9,624 characters is one old schema’s size.** Across the 12 stored cases, schemas were **5,652–10,056 characters** and user messages **13,202–134,199 characters**. The 36 scored calls used **5,534–38,797 input tokens** each. Copying the evidence enum adds up to about **2,332 serialized characters** before new descriptions, and the enum already appears in `cites` and `pushes`. Use one schema definition for passage IDs if the strict request path accepts it. Report whole-request characters, estimated tokens and maximum size before and after. Write the actual field descriptions into the design for review now. Keep the paid examples as tests and use examples from other domains in the request. [Size proposal](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/REPAIR-DESIGN.md:167) · [Repeated passage enum](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/render_v13.py:61) · [Prompt size rule](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/AGENTS.md:220)\n48:\n49:**Recommendation:** bring back one revised design with the exact schema wording and corrected case list. The decisive $0 test is to send the registered good and bad forms through the same schema, converter and checks the gate uses, then read the blind writers’ first answers against those same rules and passages.\n1:# Round 49, Claude's reflection after Codex's design review (written before round 50)\n2:\n3:## What round 49 showed\n4:\n5:- **The repair is not bounded.** Codex walked all 58 refusal templates in `calc_check.py` (893 lines). Rules for C1 through C10 are\n6:  still unstated after the repair as designed: the component choice by form; C2's tolerance and bound direction; C3's\n7:  compare_periods; C4's path-through-the-forecast-node rule; C5's reference graph; C6's overlap semantics; C7's arity limits; C8's\n8:  operand rules per operation; C9's dependence test.\n9:- **Two of my four check changes are unsound.**\n10:  - The \"vs\" growth label lets the answer supply its own proof. A 12% sequential rate relabelled as year over year passes.\n11:  - The containment rule lets \"not be over $300 billion\" pass as \"> $300B\", and \"-12%\" pass as \"+12%\".\n12:- **My test inputs were wrong.** old-2 v41 is a Fed question, and S is CY2026, not CY2027. The pair and O reuse almost all of the\n13:  gate's evidence.\n14:- **Part B could \"pass\" with zero usable answers.** All-unresolved answers pass every check.\n15:\n16:## The pattern (AGENTS §1, §7.3: \"When one mechanism draws findings round after round, stop answering findings: propose one structural rule\")\n17:\n18:- The contract grew over 30 design rounds (46 to 46ad) and the 8 harness rounds. Most of its rules came from answering one gate\n19:  fixture's finding at a time: R1 to R4, G1 to G4, the relabel and route variants. `calc_check.py` carries 29 council-tagged rules.\n20:- Each rule is identity or arithmetic, as D725 asked. Together, though, they form a 58-way conjunction over about 20 figures per\n21:  answer.\n22:- **The rules were never written for the model.** The $0 gate tested them only against answers written by someone who knew them.\n23:- **Stating every rule** would turn the schema into a manual. It would also not change the conjunction: luna (medium effort, one pass)\n24:  must satisfy all of them at once. Round 48's reflection already warned that a long conjunction fails by chance even when each part\n25:  is usually right.\n26:- **What this means for D734's hypothesis.** The hypothesis was that \"a bounded repair (descriptions, enums and four check fixes)\n27:  makes the format statable\". Round 49 contradicts it. By D355, the line stops and goes back to Alex.\n28:\n29:## What still serves D725's goal\n30:\n31:D725's diagnosis was three things:\n32:- related questions have no shared, checked calculation;\n33:- a prose answer cannot be checked;\n34:- each question is judged alone, on split inputs.\n35:\n36:The goal is that the numbers a member publishes are consistent with each other and with the passages. These checks carry that goal\n37:directly:\n38:1. A source number appears in the cited quote.\n39:2. Arithmetic is right.\n40:3. A part's outcome does not exceed its whole's.\n41:4. A shared quarter has one forecast that both members use.\n42:5. A rung contains its outcome.\n43:6. A reference is not the system's own estimate.\n44:\n45:The rest are refinements: typed relations, bridges and `converts`, roll-forward formats, per-unit identities, scenario membership\n46:along paths, C9's perturbation test and the exact-expression grammar. Each was added to stop a case a reviewer constructed.\n47:Arguably they are better shown to the readers as recorded assumptions than enforced as refusals: the design already records\n48:overlaps, adjustments and bridges for readers, not refusals.\n49:\n50:## Options for Alex (my lean, to be challenged in round 50)\n51:\n52:- **(A) Continue the full repair.** State all 58 rules and fix the unsound changes, which means several more council rounds. The risk\n53:  stays that luna cannot meet the conjunction even when every rule is stated.\n54:- **(B) Shrink the format to the core six checks** (above), with a short schema that states each one. The other structure is kept as\n55:  recorded assumptions for the readers, never as refusals. Then rebuild the $0 gate and the blind test on the smaller format.\n56:  - This is a new design step inside judging-v13, not a wording fix.\n57:  - It would be measured by the same 36-request gate, with D725's lines unchanged.\n58:- **(C) Close judging-v13.** poc-v118 stays the product, and the number answers stay a known defect.\n59:\n60:My lean is (B). It is the structural rule: enforce only what the goal needs, state every enforced rule beside its field, and show the\n61:rest to readers. What would refute it: the six checks cannot be stated in a short schema without the refinements creeping back, or a\n62:blind writer still needs unstated rules.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_12","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'nl -ba docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/REPAIR-DESIGN.md'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_12","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'nl -ba docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/REPAIR-DESIGN.md'","aggregated_output":"     1\t# judging-v13 repair: every field's rule beside its field, the checks that refuse right answers, and a $0 blind conformance test (D734)\n     2\t\n     3\tAlex chose one bounded repair, then a $0 blind test (D734; council round 48, D733). This page is the design, and it goes to the council\n     4\tbefore any code. **Out of scope:**\n     5\t- the frozen ruler and the production configuration;\n     6\t- D725's pass lines and D731's harness rules (scoring, journals, packets, verdict);\n     7\t- the system text's judging sentences.\n     8\t\n     9\t**The structural rule of this repair:** a rule code enforces is either held by the schema (an enum, a typed marker) or written in\n    10\tthe schema beside its field. The check never refuses on a rule the request does not state (AGENTS §3, \"Code over prose\"; §6, \"a request\n    11\tnever asks for what a check refuses\"; \"a field's definition sits in the schema beside the field\").\n    12\t\n    13\t## 1. The answer shape: links that failed become enums, id fields say they hold ids\n    14\t\n    15\tEvery field the model filled with prose has a name that reads as content: `outcome`, `reference.figure`, `bridge` and `evidence`. Of\n    16\tthe 90 member answers, 0 named a figure as their outcome. `inputs`, whose content is plainly a list of figures, held prose once in 72\n    17\tanswers.\n    18\t\n    19\t| Today (schema field) | Repaired | Enforced by |\n    20\t|---|---|---|\n    21\t| `answers[].outcome` (free string) | **`figures[].outcome_for`**: an array of member ids (enum). The figure marked for a member is that member's outcome | the schema enum; code checks exactly one marked figure per `forecast` member and none for an `unresolved` one (C7) |\n    22\t| `answers[].reference {figure, unavailable}` | **`figures[].reference_for`**: an array of member ids (enum), plus **`answers[].reference_unavailable`**: nullable string | the schema enum; code checks exactly one of a marked figure or a reason (C5) |\n    23\t| `figures[].evidence` (free string) | **`evidence_id`**: an enum of the request's passage ids, or null | the schema enum (the request already uses this enum for `cites`) |\n    24\t| `figures[].bridge` | **`bridge_estimate_id`**: the id of an estimate in `figures`; the assumption's words go in that estimate's `basis_text` | the description, then C7 and C8 |\n    25\t| `figures[].disputed` | **`disputed_input_id`** | the description, then C8 |\n    26\t| `figures[].converts` | **`converts_input_ids`** | the description, then C8 |\n    27\t| `figures[].adjusts.figure` | **`adjusts.figure_id`** (a figure, never a passage) | the description, then C7 |\n    28\t| `figures[].unit` (free string) | **enum `USD bn`, `USD`, `%`, `x`, `units`** | the schema enum |\n    29\t| `figures[].scenario` (free string; `range` is special in the check) | **enum `base`, `low`, `high`, `range`** | the schema enum |\n    30\t\n    31\t**One owner for the conversion.** `calc_check.from_answer(data)` converts the schema's shape into the shape the checks read, and\n    32\treports the marker errors as C7 and C5. It replaces `strip`/`without_nulls` at both call sites (`paid_gate.Gate.attempt`,\n    33\t`render_v13`). The $0 gate's fixtures stay in the internal shape. The rehearsal's stub answers move to the schema's shape.\n    34\t\n    35\t## 2. Each field's rule, beside the field (descriptions to be added; exact text is set in the build and read in review)\n    36\t\n    37\t| Field | The rule the check holds today | Where the request states it after the repair |\n    38\t|---|---|---|\n    39\t| `id` | unique; referenced by the id fields | \"A short id, unique in this list (for example F1); other fields name figures by it.\" |\n    40\t| `what.period` | `_quarters` reads `FY2027`, `FY2027 Q1`–`Q4`, `FY2027 H1`/`H2`; roll_forward needs `as of <date>` and `end of <period>`; growth periods (§3.2) | a closed list with examples: \"FY2027\", \"FY2027 Q3\", \"FY2027 H1\", \"as of 2026-07-26\", \"end of FY2027\", \"CY2027\", \"FY2027 Q4 vs FY2026 Q4\" (a growth rate) |\n    41\t| `what.measure` | per-unit identity needs `price per unit` and `units`; outcome keys must match exactly | added: \"a per-unit price is measure 'price per unit' (unit USD), a count is 'units'; an outcome uses its member's key exactly\" |\n    42\t| `value`, `low`, `high`, `bound` | exactly one of a value or an ordered range; a bound qualifies a value | \"Either value (with bound when the figure is 'more than', 'about' …) or low and high, never both. '$108.0 billion, plus or minus 2%' is two source figures from one expression, midpoint 108 and relative 2 (%); its range is a computed range_from_relative.\" |\n    43\t| `unit` | `USD bn`, `USD`, `%`, `x`, `units` | the enum, and: \"money in billions of US dollars ($1.05 trillion = 1050; $500 million = 0.5); a percentage as its number (92.5% = 92.5)\" |\n    44\t| `evidence_id`, `expression`, `component` | the expression is copied from the passage's quote and contains one complete numeric expression (§3.4) | \"source only: the passage id; the expression copied word for word from that item's Quote line, never its summary\" |\n    45\t| `inputs` | ids of figures; the operation's arity | \"computed only: ids of figures in this list (never passage ids), in the operation's order\" |\n    46\t| `operation` | arity and meaning (`_compute`) | each operation with its operands: sum(a, b, …) with signs; difference(a, b) = a − b; product(a, b, …) of multipliers (x) or units × price; ratio(a, b) = a / b; growth(old, new) in %; apply_growth(base, growth %); share(whole, share %); range_from_relative(midpoint, relative %); roll_forward(opening, flows …) with signs |\n    47\t| `signs` | sum and roll_forward only, one ±1 per input | \"sum and roll_forward only: one 1 or −1 per input, in order (a − b − c is sum with signs [1, −1, −1]); otherwise null\" |\n    48\t| `relation` | as today | kept, and the disputed operand named by `disputed_input_id` |\n    49\t| `bridge_estimate_id`, `converts_input_ids` | a bridge is an estimate whose `converts` equals the differing operands | \"only when the inputs measure something other than the output and no identity in `operation` covers it: the id of an estimate figure whose converts_input_ids lists exactly those inputs and whose basis_text states the assumption\" |\n    50\t| `basis_text`, `rests_on`, `adjusts` | estimate only | \"estimate only: the assumption in words; the ids of figures and passages it rests on; the figure it changes and how\" |\n    51\t| `outcome_for`, `reference_for` | §1 | \"the member(s) whose forecast this figure is (exactly one per forecast member)\"; \"the member(s) for which this figure is the market's reference: a consensus, guidance or outside figure, or one computed only from those and reported figures\" |\n    52\t| `rung`, `reasons`, `why`, `reference_unavailable` | C6, C5 | \"the rung whose band contains the outcome\"; \"unresolved only: why\"; \"only when no reference figure exists: why\" |\n    53\t\n    54\t## 3. Checks that refuse right answers (each found on a stored answer; each change keeps the refusal it was built for)\n    55\t\n    56\t| # | Stored right answer refused | Today's rule | Change | Still refused |\n    57\t|---|---|---|---|---|\n    58\t| 3.1 | Q1 + Q2 Data Center revenue = \"FY2027 first half\" | `_quarters` reads `H1` only | no check change; the period list is stated (§2) and other labels are refused with a message that names the list | periods that overlap or do not form the output |\n    59\t| 3.2 | FY2026 Q4 × (1 + the source's 65% year-over-year growth) = FY2027 Q4 | `apply_growth` needs a bridge unless the family declares `same_base` | a growth figure states its comparison in its period, \"FY2027 Q4 vs FY2026 Q4\". `apply_growth(base, g)` needs no bridge when base and output are one quantity (population, measure, scope) and g's period is \"<output period> vs <base period>\". The `growth` operation's output takes the same form | a rate applied to another base period (a Q3 sequential rate on FY2026 Q4) without a bridge |\n    60\t| 3.3 | $411 billion × 92.5% computed as 38,017.5 | `product` multiplies the raw number | `product` refuses a `%` operand with \"use share (whole, share %)\"; `share` is described | a percentage multiplied as a plain number |\n    61\t| 3.4 | \"well over $300 billion\", \"65% growth in Q4\" | the expression must equal an extracted expression | the expression must be a verbatim substring of the quote and contain exactly one extracted expression, matched by position; that expression decides value, unit, bound and component | a stripped qualifier (\"$300 billion\" from \"well over $300 billion\"); an expression holding two numbers; an expression not in the quote |\n    62\t| 3.5 | $1.05 trillion compared with bands in billions | free unit string | the unit enum and its description (§2) | — |\n    63\t\n    64\t**Not changed:**\n    65\t- scope changes still need a bridge;\n    66\t- an `overlap_unknown` is recorded, not refused;\n    67\t- cycles are refused;\n    68\t- a summary is not a source;\n    69\t- C3, C4, C9 and C10 are unchanged.\n    70\t\n    71\t## 4. Tables\n    72\t\n    73\t**Value × reader.**\n    74\t- **The schema** (`render_v13.v13_schema`) is read by `paid_gate.item`, which rebuilds the plan (a new pinned plan under a new dated\n    75\t  name; `paid-plan.json` and its run stay as the record), and by `render_v13.main`.\n    76\t- **The converted answer** (`from_answer`) is read by `cc.check`, `cc.publish_decision`, `paid_gate.summarise` (merge), `packets`\n    77\t  (`card_view`, `normalise`) and the verdict.\n    78\t- **The fields renamed in §1** are read by none of these after conversion: the checks keep their internal names.\n    79\t- **`model.check_schema`** validates the enums before any check.\n    80\t\n    81\t**New path × old checks.**\n    82\t- The $0 gate (87 items), the paid rehearsal (63 checks) and both mutant sets run again. The internal-shape fixtures are unchanged.\n    83\t- The stub answers in the schema's shape are rewritten to the new shape.\n    84\t- A new mutant for each change in §3 shows its test fails without it.\n    85\t\n    86\t**State × event (the blind test harness).**\n    87\t\n    88\t| Event | Outcome |\n    89\t|---|---|\n    90\t| a writer's output is not JSON, or fails the schema | recorded as a writer refusal on a stated rule (the schema), with the raw text |\n    91\t| a refusal by the checks | classified (§5) by the sentence of the request that states the rule, or \"not stated\" |\n    92\t| a writer session fails or times out | recorded; resent once; a second failure leaves that request unwritten, and the test is INCOMPLETE |\n    93\t| a later request shares a writer session | never: one fresh session per writer, all six requests in one prompt, answered in order |\n    94\t\n    95\t**Prior decisions.**\n    96\t- D725: lines unchanged.\n    97\t- D731: harness rules unchanged.\n    98\t- D728: no earlier forecast.\n    99\t- D359, D723: no product launcher.\n   100\t- AGENTS §3 and §6.\n   101\t- D733, D734.\n   102\t- DESIGN.md §2 for the internal format; this page changes only the schema's shape and the four rules of §3.\n   103\t\n   104\t## 5. The blind conformance test (registered before it runs; $0, no provider call)\n   105\t\n   106\t**Inputs: fresh requests, not the gate's.**\n   107\t- One joint pair, old-2 v14 call 8620 and v9 call 8621, with its two separate requests.\n   108\t- Three singletons:\n   109\t  - O, old-2 v29 call 8507;\n   110\t  - S, old-3 v6 call 9043;\n   111\t  - E, old-2 v41 call 8287.\n   112\t- Six requests in all, built by the repaired `paid_gate.item` exactly as the gate builds its own.\n   113\t- These calls must differ from the gate's calls, and the build reports how much of their evidence they share with the gate's.\n   114\t\n   115\t**Part A, the checks against registered forms (code).**\n   116\t- **A1, correct forms.** Each is taken from a stored answer whose intended figure and source can be identified, written in the new\n   117\t  shape, converted, and checked inside a minimal family. Each must pass:\n   118\t  - $108.0B ± 2% as midpoint and relative, with range_from_relative;\n   119\t  - Q1 + Q2 = FY2027 H1;\n   120\t  - a year-over-year apply_growth with its \"vs\" period;\n   121\t  - company total × the Data Center mix through share;\n   122\t  - \"well over $300 billion\" and \"65% growth in Q4\" as contained expressions;\n   123\t  - a four-term residual as a sum with signs;\n   124\t  - $1.05 trillion as 1050 USD bn against bands in billions;\n   125\t  - $25 billion as 25 USD bn.\n   126\t- **A2, planted defects on copies of A1.** Each must be refused by its named check:\n   127\t  - 108.5 for $108.0B (C1);\n   128\t  - \"$300 billion\" stripped from \"well over $300 billion\" (C1);\n   129\t  - an expression holding two numbers (C1);\n   130\t  - Q2 + Q3 labelled H1 (C8);\n   131\t  - a Data Center quarter summed into company revenue (C8);\n   132\t  - a percentage through product (C2 or C8, with the share message);\n   133\t  - a growth rate on another base period with no bridge (C8);\n   134\t  - a cycle (C7);\n   135\t  - no outcome marker, and two outcome markers (C7);\n   136\t  - both a reference marker and a reason (C5).\n   137\t- **A3, the existing harness.** The $0 gate, the rehearsal and the mutants pass again.\n   138\t\n   139\t**Part B, the request read by blind writers.**\n   140\t- **Two writers:** a fresh Claude subagent and a fresh read-only Codex session. Each is given only the six requests' system text,\n   141\t  user text and schema as files, and the instruction to answer each as the JSON schema defines it.\n   142\t- **Each writer's first outputs** go through `model.check_schema`, `from_answer` and `cc.check` unedited.\n   143\t- **Every refusal is classified:**\n   144\t  - (i) the request states the rule (the sentence is quoted), so the writer erred;\n   145\t  - (ii) the request does not state it;\n   146\t  - (iii) the check refuses a right answer (read against the passages).\n   147\t\n   148\t**Stop.** The test stops on any of these:\n   149\t- any A1 form refused;\n   150\t- any A2 defect passing its check;\n   151\t- any refusal in Part B classed (ii) or (iii);\n   152\t- an INCOMPLETE run.\n   153\t\n   154\t**Reported beside the verdict:**\n   155\t- how many of the 12 writer answers pass every check;\n   156\t- the (i) refusals by rule.\n   157\t\n   158\tFewer than 6 of 12 passing is not a stop, but the council reconsiders the format's weight before any re-send is asked for. A\n   159\twriter is a stronger reader than gpt-6-luna, so a clean result shows the request states its rules. It does not show luna will\n   160\tfollow them: only the re-send measures that.\n   161\t\n   162\t**A clean result** goes to Alex as a request to re-send the same 36 requests, rebuilt with the repaired request, under a new\n   163\tdecision with D725's lines.\n   164\t\n   165\t**Cost:** $0 at the provider. The writers run as a subagent and a Codex session.\n   166\t\n   167\t**Request size:** the schema's descriptions grow it from 9,624 characters. The growth is the missing definitions, which is the\n   168\twritten reason (AGENTS §6), and the build reports characters before and after. The system text is unchanged.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_13","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'for f in codex-r48 claude-r48 codex-r49 claude-r49; do nl -ba \"docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/$f.md\"; done'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_13","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'for f in codex-r48 claude-r48 codex-r49 claude-r49; do nl -ba \"docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/$f.md\"; done'","aggregated_output":"     1\t**The paid gate hit STOP. Judging-v13 needs one blind, zero-cost test of its answer format before another paid run.** Every reply wrote a forecast in words where the checker expected a figure ID. The request never said that field needed an ID. I also found checks that rejected correct calculations, including one that turned $411 billion × 92.5% into $38,017.5 billion. The run establishes that this version could publish nothing. It leaves open whether judging related questions together improves the forecasts. [Paid gate, §4](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/PAID-GATE.md:250)\n     2\t\n     3\t## 1. Census\n     4\t\n     5\tI matched all 72 finished entries to their requests and reproduced every recorded refusal in memory. Each journal also contained 36 `started` markers. The first attempts cost $0.155358 and the retries cost $0.212361, for **$0.367719 total**. All 36 first attempts and all 36 retries were refused. That gives the joint arm **0 of 9 usable pairs** against the required 8 of 9. [First attempts](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13-paid-gate-2026-11-22/answers.jsonl) · [Retries](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13-paid-gate-2026-11-22/retries.jsonl)\n     6\t\n     7\tEach check cell shows **refusal messages / replies affected**. A reply can have several messages.\n     8\t\n     9\t| Attempt | Arm | Replies | C7 structure | C1 source | C2 arithmetic or bounds | C8 compatible inputs | Stopped at first structure check |\n    10\t|---|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|\n    11\t| First | Joint | 9 | 39 / 9 | 0 | 0 | 0 | 9 |\n    12\t| First | Separate | 18 | 47 / 18 | 8 / 1 | 0 | 0 | 17 |\n    13\t| First | Singletons | 9 | 13 / 9 | 3 / 1 | 0 | 0 | 8 |\n    14\t| Retry | Joint | 9 | 40 / 9 | 0 | 0 | 0 | 9 |\n    15\t| Retry | Separate | 18 | 41 / 18 | 25 / 4 | 6 / 3 | 14 / 4 | 14 |\n    16\t| Retry | Singletons | 9 | 12 / 9 | 7 / 3 | 1 / 1 | 2 / 2 | 6 |\n    17\t\n    18\t- **C7, first → retry.** Undefined figure references 51 → 45, point/range violations 28 → 24, wrong operand counts 9 → 9, invalid signs 9 → 7, missing outcome links 2 → 7, cycles 0 → 1. All 52 point/range violations supplied both a point and a range. The checker stopped 63 replies before checking source figures. It also skipped the rung and reference checks when it could not find the outcome figure. That explains why only nine missing-outcome messages were logged even though all 72 replies had that problem. [Structure and early return](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/calc_check.py:302) · [Outcome, rung and reference checks](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/calc_check.py:468)\n    19\t- **Other classes.** C1 first → retry was 8 → 10 unrecognized evidence references, 2 → 6 expression mismatches, and 1 → 16 value/unit/component/bound mismatches. The retries exposed five C2 arithmetic and two bound refusals, plus ten C8 bridge, three period, two share and one overlap refusal. C3–C6 and C9–C10 had zero recorded messages. Among singletons, equity commitments produced all three first-attempt C1 messages. On retry, equity commitments produced five C1, one C2 and one C8. Vera CPU produced two C1 and one C8. Hyperscaler spending stopped at C7 in all six replies. [First attempts](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13-paid-gate-2026-11-22/answers.jsonl) · [Retries](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13-paid-gate-2026-11-22/retries.jsonl)\n    20\t\n    21\tI counted nonempty values at the named fields against the exact IDs available to that reply. The table counts **occurrences**, so a reply with two forecasts can contribute two outcome entries.\n    22\t\n    23\t| Field containing prose | First joint / separate / single | First total | Retry joint / separate / single | Retry total |\n    24\t|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|\n    25\t| `figures[].evidence` | 67 / 96 / 12 | 175 | 35 / 88 / 10 | 133 |\n    26\t| `figures[].bridge` | 25 / 18 / 6 | 49 | 23 / 17 / 4 | 44 |\n    27\t| `answers[].outcome` | 18 / 18 / 9 | 45 | 18 / 18 / 9 | 45 |\n    28\t| `answers[].reference.figure` | 16 / 15 / 8 | 39 | 12 / 13 / 7 | 32 |\n    29\t| `figures[].converts[]` | 0 / 0 / 2 | 2 | 0 / 0 / 0 | 0 |\n    30\t| `figures[].disputed` | 0 / 0 / 1 | 1 | 0 / 0 / 2 | 2 |\n    31\t| `figures[].inputs[]` | 0 / 0 / 0 | 0 | 0 / 1 / 0 | 1 |\n    32\t\n    33\t**Corrections to Claude’s counts.** The first-attempt counts of 49 bridges, 45 outcomes, 39 references and two `converts` entries match. The 175 evidence fields hold prose, but only **139** are source figures where C1 requires a passage ID. The retry split is **95 source figures out of 133**. The one `adjusts.figure` entry is `e2652`, a real passage ID in a field that needs a figure ID. The same value also appears in `inputs[]` on that figure. Claude missed the `disputed` prose in the equity-commitment first attempt. Two retry `converts[]` entries likewise use passage IDs where operand IDs are expected. Finally, the four units Claude lists are the source parser’s units. The checker also uses `units` for per-unit calculations, and it has no general unit check for estimates. [First-attempt wrong ID](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13-paid-gate-2026-11-22/answers.jsonl:6) · [First-attempt overlap prose](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13-paid-gate-2026-11-22/answers.jsonl:68) · [Source units](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/calc_check.py:45) · [Per-unit calculation](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/calc_check.py:665)\n    34\t\n    35\t## 2. What the refusals mean\n    36\t\n    37\t**A** and **R** refer to line numbers in the first-attempt and retry journals. I read the figures and their inputs against the passages in the actual request. “Exposed on replay” means an earlier refusal hid the check in the original run. The verdict in each row applies to that refusal. [First attempts](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13-paid-gate-2026-11-22/answers.jsonl) · [Retries](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13-paid-gate-2026-11-22/retries.jsonl) · [Requests as sent](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/paid-plan.json)\n    38\t\n    39\t| Reply | Figure and evidence | Rule and finding |\n    40\t|---|---|---|\n    41\t| **A2, joint** | Q3 guidance **$108B, range $105.84–110.16B**. `e2652` says “$108.0 billion, plus or minus 2%.” Both the midpoint and endpoints are correct. | **(b) Unstated format.** C7 says “either a value or a complete range.” The schema accepts all three numbers together and gives no instruction to split the midpoint and range. [C7](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/calc_check.py:319) |\n    42\t| **A2, joint** | Q4 estimate **$112.3B**, from a reconstructed $68.1B base and `e5162`’s 65% growth. `bridge` explains that conversion in a sentence. | **(b) Unstated format.** C7 says the sentence “is not defined.” The schema calls `bridge` “an estimate stating the conversion” and never says to put the ID of a separate estimate there. [Sent schema](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/paid-plan.json:237) · [C7](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/calc_check.py:310) |\n    43\t| **A2, joint** | Outcome **“$398.1 billion”** corresponds to the reply’s own annual figure. Its quarterly inputs are $81.62B, $96.2B, $108B and $112.3B, based on `e111`, `e2649`, `e2652` and the Q4 estimate above. | **(b) Unstated format, exposed on replay.** C7 says the outcome “is not a figure.” The schema gives `outcome` as a nullable string with no description. [Sent schema](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/paid-plan.json:360) · [C7](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/calc_check.py:488) |\n    44\t| **A6, separate** | The reference reads **“dc_fy27_reference: Morningstar estimates $361 billion”**. The named source figure exists and `e2295` states $361B for FY2027 Data Center revenue. | **(b) Unstated format, masked in the original.** C5 would say “the reference names no figure” because the lookup uses the entire string. The schema describes the reference as “an external figure” without telling the writer to return only its ID. [Sent schema](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/paid-plan.json:380) · [C5](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/calc_check.py:779) |\n    45\t| **A6, separate** | The **$113.6B** Q3 estimate uses the outside forecast in `e5056` and the $108B guidance in `e2652`. It puts `e2652` in both `inputs` and `adjusts.figure`. | **(b) Unstated format.** C7 reports twice that `e2652` “is not defined.” It is defined as evidence. The schema fails to distinguish a source passage ID from a figure ID in those fields. [C7](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/calc_check.py:310) |\n    46\t| **A12, separate** | Q4 residual **$125.2B** equals $411B − $81.6B − $96.2B − $108B, from `e4097`, `e238`, `e2497` and `e2499`. The model used `difference` with four inputs and signs. | **(b) Unstated operation rules.** C7 says “difference takes 2 operands, not 4” and limits `signs` to “a sum or roll_forward.” The schema lists the operation names without their operand or sign rules. This verdict covers the arithmetic representation. Treating the full-year consensus residual as a forecast is a separate judgement. [C7](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/calc_check.py:327) |\n    47\t| **A44, equity singleton** | **`25 USD billions`**, cited to `e2620`, which reports $25B in commitments as of July 26. | **(b) Unstated unit spelling.** C1 says the value “does not equal ‘$25 billion’ as component value.” It compares the unit string to `USD bn`. The amount agrees and `unit` was a free string. [C1](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/calc_check.py:85) |\n    48\t| **A2, joint** | **65% FY2027 Q4 growth**. The reply copied “65% growth in Q4” directly from `e5162`. | **(b) Unstated span boundary, exposed on replay.** C1 says the phrase “is not a complete numeric expression” because it extracts `65%` alone. With the passage ID and `%` set, changing only the expression to `65%` cleared C1. The request did not explain where wording around a number must end. [C1](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/calc_check.py:78) |\n    49\t| **A24, Vera CPU singleton** | **$42B**, with a **$40–50B** forecast range. Its input was the threshold of more than $40B, calculated from the $20B base and “more than double” in `e3660` and `e4856`. | **(b) Unstated format.** C7 says “either a value or a complete range.” Reporting a central estimate alongside a range is reasonable under the schema as sent. Whether $42B is a supported forecast still needs a reader. [C7](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/calc_check.py:319) |\n    50\t| **A2, joint** | First-half Data Center revenue **$164.25B**, from Q1 $75.25B (`e109`) plus Q2 $89B (`e2650`). | **(c) Check too narrow, exposed on replay.** C8 requires “one quantity over disjoint periods forming FY2027 first half.” The periods do that. `_quarters` recognizes `H1` and cannot read `first half`. [Period parser](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/calc_check.py:285) · [C8](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/calc_check.py:411) |\n    51\t| **A4, separate** | FY2027 Q4 **$112.2B** from $68B FY2026 Q4 actual (`e2452`) × 1.65, using the stated 65% year-over-year forecast (`e5162`). | **(c) Check too narrow, exposed on replay.** C8 reports “no bridge naming those operands.” It gives `apply_growth` an exception tied to a declared `same_base` family relation. The source already states this particular year-over-year link. Carrying a Q3 sequential rate into Q4 would be a different case and would still need an assumption. [C8](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/calc_check.py:434) |\n    52\t| **A8, joint** | Data Center reference proxy **$380.175B**, from $411B FY2027 company consensus (`e4097`) × the reported 92.5% Q2 mix (`e3216`). | **(c) Wrong arithmetic check, exposed on replay.** C2 says `product` “gives 38017.50.” It multiplies by 92.5 as a plain number, while `share` divides by 100. The arithmetic is correct. Applying a Q2 mix to a full-year reference remains an assumption. [Arithmetic](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/calc_check.py:125) · [C2](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/calc_check.py:371) |\n    53\t| **R12, separate** | **`> $300B`** for Data Center revenue. `e2115` says “well over $300 billion.” The reply kept both the words and the lower bound. | **(c) Check too narrow.** C1 says the quoted phrase “is not a complete numeric expression.” Its parser finds `over $300 billion` by starting one word later. It penalizes the reply for preserving the full qualifier. [Parser](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/calc_check.py:12) · [C1](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/calc_check.py:78) |\n    54\t| **R66, separate** | Annual Data Center calculation **$375.788B**, from the first six months’ $164B (`e5360`) plus $99.9B and $111.888B for Q3 and Q4. The later quarters use the guidance and mix in `e2715`, `e3453` and `e4190`. | **(c) Check too narrow.** C8 requires “disjoint periods forming FY2027.” On the stored reply, changing only `FY2027 first six months` to `FY2027 H1` removed exactly this refusal. The calculation’s other refusals remained. [C8](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/calc_check.py:411) |\n    55\t| **R44, equity singleton** | **$46.5B** = $25B + $18B + $3.5B. The reply puts a sentence about MediaTek’s overlap in `disputed`. `e2620` gives the July balance. `e2889` says $18B is on top of **$47.9B already held**, which does not establish that it is on top of the **$25B already committed**. | **(b) Unstated format for the recorded refusal.** C8 says “overlap_unknown must name a disputed operand.” The schema does not say to provide that operand’s ID. **(a) Separate judgement gap.** Changing the prose to the MediaTek figure ID would leave the addition of $25B and $18B resting on an undeclared assumption. [C8](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/calc_check.py:418) |\n    56\t| **R60, separate** | The **15%** Q4 growth estimate rests on the **$124.2B** company and **$114.885B** Data Center forecasts, both computed using that same 15%. `e3222` supports reacceleration qualitatively and gives no rate. | **(a) Model error.** C7 says “the figure graph has a cycle.” The forecast figures cannot serve as independent support for the rate that produces them. Renaming fields leaves the circular reasoning intact. [C7](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/calc_check.py:341) |\n    57\t| **R22, hyperscaler singleton** | **$1.05 trillion** in rung 3, based on a judgemental discount to the $1.3T projection and the $1.08T consensus (`e4756`, `e1926`). | **(c) Wrong unit comparison, exposed on replay.** After resolving the outcome link and setting aside the prose bridge on a copy, C6 says the $900–1,100B band “contains no part” of `1.05`. Converting the dollar figures to billions cleared the remaining checks. The reply itself says neither scope adjustment was quantified. A code pass still leaves the reader to decide whether the adjustment is supported. [C6](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/calc_check.py:495) |\n    58\t\n    59\tOne more source boundary matters for the proposed fix. R44’s **US$3.5B** appears in the evidence summary for `e3558`, but the attached quote only describes the convertible bonds. `e3474`, also shown, has the amount in its quote. The model sees both summaries and quotes. C1 checks only the quote attached to the selected ID. The request needs to say which text can support a source figure. A match on the amount alone cannot settle that. [Request evidence and quote handling](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/paid_gate.py:55) · [C1](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/calc_check.py:78)\n    60\t\n    61\tI reproduced Claude’s diagnostic **0 publishes** and **9 replies with no C7** on the first attempts. The retries give the same counts. The phrase “past the structural layer” needs a correction. **25 of 36** in each journal passed the *early* structural check after the script’s edits. Later outcome-link failures reduced that to nine without any C7. The script also guesses links from matching amounts, deletes prose bridges and drops valid range endpoints when a midpoint is present. Its default options omit the `prose` pass used to reproduce these counts. These are useful probes for finding hidden checks. They do not establish how many replies a repaired request would get right, or that most of the 72 replies had sound forecasts. [Diagnostic script](/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-lchingen-hivemind/f1ea972d-85a1-4f27-8010-cf0b60054109/scratchpad/normalise_diag.py:12)\n    62\t\n    63\t## 3. Why the zero-cost gate missed it\n    64\t\n    65\t**The author of the passing answer already knew the checker’s rules.** The fixtures hand in the expected IDs, units and period spellings. The paid rehearsal’s clean answer goes further and uses an estimate inside a preselected rung, with no source calculation and an unavailable reference. That proves the harness can carry a conforming answer and that planted defects can trigger checks. It leaves unanswered whether someone reading the exact request could write a conforming answer without that inside knowledge. [Registered controls](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/DESIGN.md:152) · [Clean rehearsal fixture](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/paid_rehearsal.py:42)\n    66\t\n    67\tThe missed step was a **blind write-and-check using the exact outgoing request**. Give an independent writer only the system text, user text and schema. Have them encode real forecasts and then run their first outputs through the actual harness. For each refusal, ask the writer to point to where the request stated the rule. Read the check back in the other direction for correct answers it cannot represent. An evidence ID enum would have prevented the most common source mistake. The request already uses one for citations. [AGENTS §6](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/AGENTS.md:231) · [AGENTS §7.3](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/AGENTS.md:370) · [Sent schema](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/paid-plan.json:120)\n    68\t\n    69\t## 4. One recommendation and one test\n    70\t\n    71\t**Recommendation.** Keep the STOP and ask Alex to open one bounded repair of the judging-v13 request and checker. Put each field’s rule next to the field. Use enums for known IDs, units and choices where the schema can enforce them. Fix the demonstrated arithmetic, period and source-boundary failures while preserving checks for genuine scope changes, unsupported assumptions and circular calculations. Code already extracts numeric expressions for verification. Sending a complete catalog of those expressions to the model could remove some copying, but would also make today’s parser omissions into missing evidence. It would leave the universal outcome-link failure untouched. Closing v13 now would save further work, but the result has not answered D725’s question about shared calculations. One bounded test can determine whether there is a workable way to express those calculations at all. [D725](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/DECISIONS-log.md:10957) · [D690](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/DECISIONS-log.md:10360)\n    72\t\n    73\t**Test.** Run one blind conformance test at $0 on fresh examples covering a joint pair, its two separate replies, and all three singleton calculation shapes. Before the test, register correct examples and deliberately broken counterparts, including a wrong number, a wrong period or scope, an unresolved overlap and a circular calculation. Run the independent writer’s first replies through the real harness without hand edits. **Stop if any correct form is refused, any planted defect gets through its intended check, or the reader still needs an unwritten rule to encode a correct answer.** Use the 72 stored replies to check individual fixes where the intended figure and source are identifiable. A guessed link or rewritten answer earns no credit. A clean result earns a request to rerun the same 36-call comparison under a fresh decision, with the original pass lines. The current gate’s STOP stands. [Registered reading and stop rules](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/PAID-GATE.md:250)     1\t# Round 48, Claude's own reflection (written before reading Codex's report)\n     2\t\n     3\t## The result as registered\n     4\t\n     5\t- 36 scored first attempts, 36 diagnostic retries, $0.3677 spent of $0.50 (ledger: 72 attempts, no refusal by the ledger, not stopped).\n     6\t- All 72 answers were refused by `calc_check.py`; nothing was published. Every attempt finished (status `answered`), so the result is\n     7\t  complete, not INCOMPLETE.\n     8\t- By D725's lines: the joint arm is usable in 0 of 9 pairs. **STOP.**\n     9\t- The blind reading cannot change this. The registered reader brief says a card with no answer cannot be usable, and every card is\n    10\t  \"no answer\". The readers are not run; the packets can still be rendered and sealed at no cost if the record needs them.\n    11\t\n    12\t## What the result measures\n    13\t\n    14\tIt does not measure the question the gate was built for (does judging a family in one call give better numbers than judging each\n    15\tmember alone?). It measures whether the model can fill v13's answer format as the request states it. It cannot.\n    16\t\n    17\t## The mechanism (traced on stored answers, both journals)\n    18\t\n    19\t1. **The format's rules live in the check, not in the request.** The sent schema has 7 descriptions (`what`, `basis`, `kind`,\n    20\t   `expression`, `relation`, `bridge`, `reference`). These fields have none: `value`, `low`, `high`, `bound`, `unit`, `evidence`,\n    21\t   `component`, `inputs`, `operation`, `signs`, `disputed`, `basis_text`, `rests_on`, `converts`, `scenario`, and `outcome` and `rung` on\n    22\t   the answers. The check enforces closed conventions on them that the request never states:\n    23\t   - `evidence` is a passage id, while the model wrote the sentence (175 figures in the first attempts);\n    24\t   - `outcome` and `reference.figure` are figure ids, while the model wrote \"$398.1 billion\" and sentences (45 and 39);\n    25\t   - `bridge` is a figure id, while its own description reads as prose (\"an estimate stating the conversion …\"), and the model\n    26\t     wrote prose (49);\n    27\t   - `unit` must be exactly `USD bn`, `%`, `x` or `USD`; the model wrote `USD billions`, `$ billion`, `percent` and `fraction`;\n    28\t   - a figure has a value or a range, never both; a guidance \"$108.0 billion, plus or minus 2%\" was read as value, low, high,\n    29\t     bound `approx` and component `midpoint`;\n    30\t   - a period is labelled `FY2027 H1`, not \"FY2027 first half\" or \"first six months\";\n    31\t   - a percentage is applied with `share` (which divides by 100), not `product`;\n    32\t   - `range_from_relative` takes the midpoint and the relative figure as two operands;\n    33\t   - `signs` exists only on `sum` and `roll_forward`, and a four-term difference is a `sum` with signs.\n    34\t2. **Even with these corrected mechanically on copies, nothing publishes.** 9 of 36 first attempts get past the structural layer, and\n    35\t   0 publish. The next layer includes:\n    36\t   - the expression copied with its words (\"65% growth in Q4\", where the extractor yields \"65%\");\n    37\t   - the period vocabulary;\n    38\t   - `apply_growth` of last year's quarter by a year-over-year growth. The check accepts this without a bridge only when the family\n    39\t     has a `same_base` relation, and the bridge description's list of identities does not include growth;\n    40\t   - some arithmetic that follows from the conventions above.\n    41\t3. **One wrong answer anywhere refuses the whole answer.** An answer carries about 20 figures, and each figure must pass several\n    42\t   rules. A long conjunction fails by chance even when each part is usually right (AGENTS §7.1, \"a conjunction over many items fails\n    43\t   by chance\"). The retries carried every reason, and they fixed nothing: 36 of 36 were refused again, mostly on the same classes (prose where an id\n    44\t   is expected in 20, value and range in 22).\n    45\t\n    46\t## My process error\n    47\t\n    48\t- AGENTS §6: \"Check before the send, at no cost. Write one answer that follows the prompt and run every code check on it.\"\n    49\t  - I wrote the $0 gate's answer knowing the check's conventions. It proved that the check accepts a conforming answer, not that\n    50\t    the request teaches one.\n    51\t  - The step that would have caught this at no cost: an answer written by a reader who sees only the request, run through the\n    52\t    checks.\n    53\t- REQUEST-AUDIT.md says \"field definitions moved to the schema\". Only 7 descriptions reached the sent schema, and I did not check the\n    54\t  sent bytes against that sentence.\n    55\t- The eight council rounds (47 to 47h) reviewed the harness: the journals, the keys, the packets and the verdict. None reviewed the\n    56\t  request as the model reads it against the check.\n    57\t- The \"rests_on prose\" diagnosis I gave Alex mid-run was wrong in its field. The prose sat in `bridge`, `evidence`, `outcome` and\n    58\t  `reference`, not in `rests_on`. I correct it in the report.\n    59\t\n    60\t## The way forward (my lean, to be challenged)\n    61\t\n    62\t- The gate as registered says STOP. It is recorded as written (D680).\n    63\t- What the result can still decide: whether a v13-style calculation can be filled at all. That decides whether the number-answer\n    64\t  work continues on this path.\n    65\t- Lean:\n    66\t  - **(ii) first:** move every decision code can make out of the model.\n    67\t    - Code extracts each numeric expression from the shown passages and lists it as a numbered source figure, with value, unit,\n    68\t      bound and period parsed by code. The model only references these by id. This removes the expression, evidence, unit and\n    69\t      value-or-range classes at their owner.\n    70\t    - `outcome`, `reference.figure`, `bridge`, `inputs` and `evidence` become enums of the ids the request holds where the strict\n    71\t      schema allows it.\n    72\t  - **(i):** every remaining field gets its rule in the schema beside it, including the operation and period vocabulary, and the\n    73\t    check's too-narrow rules are written down as decisions before any change.\n    74\t  - **The test, all at $0 before any send:**\n    75\t    - every stored answer is re-read under the changed contract, and no right answer may be refused;\n    76\t    - a blind reader who sees only the new request writes answers for three families, and they must pass;\n    77\t    - then the same 36-request gate, about $0.40, on Alex's word.\n    78\t  - **Stop condition:** if a blind reader's answers still fail on rules the request states, the format is too heavy for one call.\n    79\t    In that case close judging-v13 and keep poc-v118 (D690).\n    80\t- Not certain:\n    81\t  - (ii) is a new design piece (code builds the source figures), not a wording fix. It needs its own design tables (AGENTS §2)\n    82\t    before code.\n    83\t  - Whether Alex wants to spend more on this path is his call.\n     1\t**Round 49 finds serious issues. Revise the design before writing code.** I replayed the proposed growth rule in memory. It accepted a source’s **12% sequential growth** as **12% year-over-year growth** once the answer relabelled the rate. The repair would let the answer supply its own proof that the rate applies.\n     2\t\n     3\tI rebuilt the original plan byte for byte, checked the stored first answers and retries, and reran the existing gate at $0. All 87 baseline cases passed. The counterexamples below test the proposed rules in memory.\n     4\t\n     5\t## Blockers\n     6\t\n     7\t1. **§3.2 trusts the answer’s growth label.** I used the actual 12% sequential guidance in `e3943`, labelled it `FY2027 Q4 vs FY2026 Q4`, and applied it to a $100B prior-year base. The current check refused it. A literal implementation of §3.2 accepted $112B. The positive control has a related problem. `e5162`’s exact quote is “and 65% growth in Q4.” Its summary supplies *year over year*. Under the quote-only rule, that item alone cannot prove the comparison. The design needs to say where both periods come from for a source rate, how a computed rate gets its periods from its operands, and when an assumption is required. A2 needs the **falsely relabelled** rate as well as the honestly labelled rate applied to the wrong base. Replacing the existing `same_base` exception also broke G4 in my replay. Keeping that exception as written leaves a shortcut triggered by any `same_base` relation in the family. [Growth check](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/calc_check.py:438) · [12% source](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/paid-plan.json:8722) · [65% as shown](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/paid-plan.json:2387) · [G4](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/gate.py:261)\n     8\t\n     9\t2. **§3.4 verifies where the words occur and can still reverse their meaning.** In my replay, `not be over $300 billion` passed as `> $300B`. The contained match was `over $300 billion`. `-12% sequentially` also passed as positive `12%`. A qualifier can sit outside both spans. In `$300 billion or more`, the parser extracts an exact `$300 billion`, so both versions accept a point. “Exactly one extracted expression” can also admit a second quantity the parser misses, such as `$300 billion and 50 units`. Keep the positive controls for `well over` and the longer Q4 phrase. Register the sign, negation, trailing qualifier and second-quantity cases before settling what the check can certify. [Repair §3.4](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/REPAIR-DESIGN.md:61) · [Extractor and C1](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/calc_check.py:12)\n    10\t\n    11\t3. **The blind test currently supplies the wrong question for E, and S needs its year corrected.** `old-2/8287` exists and really is `v41`, but it asks whether the Fed will raise rates. `old-3/8287` asks about an investment. Vera CPU FY2028 is `v41` in `old-3`, where I found 20 alternatives to paid call 9166. S’s `old-3/9043` correctly asks about four hyperscalers, for **calendar 2026**. Reusing the paid S key would ask for **CY2027** in `Outcome keys`. The render path accepts a caller-supplied member ID and copies the archived heading without checking that they describe the same question. Register the archive, call ID, question, period and ladder together. [Blind inputs](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/REPAIR-DESIGN.md:104) · [Paid case declarations](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/paid_gate.py:39) · [Render path](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/render_v13.py:85)\n    12\t\n    13\t## Rules still missing from the request\n    14\t\n    15\tI walked every refusal in `calc_check.py`. The proposal covers unique figure IDs, the individual field enums, figure ID versus passage ID for the renamed fields, and the exact outcome key in substance. The existing request already prints all four parts of the outcome key. The following rules are missing, incomplete or stated differently from what code enforces. [Proposed schema descriptions](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/REPAIR-DESIGN.md:35) · [Existing outcome keys](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/render_v13.py:104)\n    16\t\n    17\t| Check | What the request still needs to say or encode |\n    18\t|---|---|\n    19\t| **C1 — source numbers** | A source must supply a shown passage ID and a matching value, unit, bound and component. State the component choices by form. A money range supplies separate `low` and `high` figures, a relative range supplies `midpoint` and `relative`, and a multiplier uses `multiplier` and keeps its qualifier. Ordinary money and percentages use `value`, and qualified figures have specific `bound` rules. The enum names the components without telling the writer when each works. [C1](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/calc_check.py:78) |\n    20\t| **C2 — arithmetic** | State the point-versus-range comparison, exact zero for cancelling terms, allowed rounding, bound direction and what to do with opposing bounds or an unevaluable calculation. Code allows a point at the computed midpoint for most operations, checks both ends for a supplied range and `range_from_relative`, and uses a 0.5% tolerance with floors of 0.05 for percentages and 0.1 otherwise. Bounds can flip on subtraction or division. Opposing directions require an estimate. [Tolerance](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/calc_check.py:280) · [Refusals](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/calc_check.py:364) |\n    21\t| **C3 — part and whole** | Render the actual `compare_periods`. The checker uses each member’s population, measure and scope with each listed period and scenario. It refuses duplicate reachable forecast figures for either side and checks the part against the whole when both exist. It compares corresponding ends for points and closed ranges. With an open bound it asks whether any pair of values can fit. The current general instruction that a part cannot exceed its whole leaves these rules out. [Comparison](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/calc_check.py:553) · [Bounds](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/calc_check.py:210) |\n    22\t| **C4 — shared figures and scenarios** | The exact shared key is all four `what` fields. When an outcome touches that key, every path to it must pass through a forecast figure, with exactly one such figure per represented scenario. A point calculation or estimate cannot borrow a shared forecast or an estimate from another scenario, including through intermediate figures. Part and whole must cover the same estimate-derived scenarios. `range` means an estimate with both ends that serves as an outcome and feeds no figure. The enum alone supplies none of these rules. [Scenario and range checks](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/calc_check.py:512) · [Shared paths](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/calc_check.py:572) |\n    23\t| **C5 — reference** | A forecast needs exactly one marked reference or a nonempty reason. The rule applies to the entire calculation behind the reference. It must contain at least one external source whose speaker is other than `system`. Every source at the bottom must be external or reported. Estimates, earlier forecasts and figures labelled `forecast` are barred anywhere in the calculation. A direct reported figure alone fails. The individual `basis` and `kind` enums do not encode these combinations. There is no general `basis × kind` cross-check elsewhere beyond the prior rule below. [Choice](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/calc_check.py:503) · [Reference graph](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/calc_check.py:779) |\n    24\t| **C6 — rung** | A rung is an integer from 1 through that member’s ladder length. Its band must **overlap** the recomputed outcome. It can contain just part of a range or bounded outcome. Lower band edges are included and upper edges excluded. If several bands overlap, the reasons should explain the choice, as the original design requires. “The band contains the outcome” reads as full containment. [C6](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/calc_check.py:495) · [Band edges](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/calc_check.py:200) · [Original design](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/DESIGN.md:75) |\n    25\t| **C7 — structure** | State that all figure links must resolve and none may form a cycle, including through `rests_on`, adjustments and bridges. State exactly one answer per member and the unresolved shape explicitly, including a nonempty reason and no rung. Specify `low ≤ high`, the actual operand limits (`sum` 1–99, `product` and `roll_forward` 2–99, other operations exactly 2), and that null `signs` means all additions. The existing user text covers one answer per member, and the member enum covers unknown member IDs. The repair still needs to define duplicate answer and marker handling. [Figure checks](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/calc_check.py:302) · [Member checks](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/calc_check.py:468) |\n    26\t| **C8 — compatible inputs** | Scope the fiscal-period spellings to time aggregation, including same quantity, same fiscal year and disjoint periods that exactly form the result. Name which `sum`/`difference` relations permit differing keys, and that `accounting_parts` needs a caller-declared scope split. The paid builder supplies no `scope_parts`. For `roll_forward`, specify the opening, flows and closing formats. For `overlap_unknown`, the disputed ID must be an actual operand. For `share`, specify one percentage and one whole, whole first. For a bridge, say which operand IDs must match exactly. The current comparison excludes `%` and `x` operands when finding differing keys, and it checks the bridge’s kind and IDs but does not require assumption text. The multiplier exception and per-unit identity need full operand rules. Per-unit `ratio` uses total then price and produces a figure whose measure is `units`. `product` uses a count and price for the same population and scope. The check accepts price in `USD` **or** `USD bn`. [Compatibility](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/calc_check.py:398) · [Per-unit identity](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/calc_check.py:665) · [Paid family data](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/paid_gate.py:63) |\n    27\t| **C9 and C10 — bases and earlier answers** | C9 requires a value-determining path to the earlier member’s published forecast or the matching scenario behind its range, with no bypass. A citation alone fails, a base that cancels fails, and multiple earlier scenarios must be covered exactly. Code tests dependence by changing the earlier midpoint by 10%. The schema says none of this. C10 and C7’s prior-pointer refusal are closed off in the current wire format by the `kind` and `basis` enums and the absence of `pointer`. Preserve the internal tests for exact member, day and value, comparison-only use, and the ban on using a prior as an outcome or input. [C9 and C10](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/calc_check.py:599) · [Prior pointer](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/calc_check.py:357) · [Wire schema](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/render_v13.py:30) |\n    28\t\n    29\t## Major findings\n    30\t\n    31\t- **The markers can represent the legitimate old links.** They can mark a range summary as an outcome, let several members share one figure, let a forecast use a computed reference, and represent an unresolved member with neither marker. Define what happens if an unresolved member is marked for a reference or given an unavailable reason. The old checker skipped references for unresolved answers. Also define repeated member IDs on one figure, competing figures, and the treatment of an invalid answer during diagnostic rendering. `from_answer` is a reasonable single place to translate the wire shape. It needs a defined result on marker errors and a path to each raw-answer reader. `Gate.attempt`, `summarise` and `card_lines` each read the raw shape independently. Both scoring and diagnostic packet rendering use `card_lines`. `strict()` is the reverse direction for the render fixture and currently drops fields with new names. [Marker design](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/REPAIR-DESIGN.md:19) · [Unresolved branch](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/calc_check.py:484) · [First answer](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/paid_gate.py:165) · [Merge input](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/paid_gate.py:266) · [Packet input](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/paid_gate.py:501) · [Fixture encoder](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/render_v13.py:116)\n    32\t\n    33\t- **The scenario enum changes what the analyst can express.** `DESIGN.md` allows named scenarios and gives low and high as examples. Two different downside scenarios can have separate assumptions and forecasts. Forcing both into `low` can cause a duplicate-forecast refusal or mix the two calculations. The internal gate even uses `alt` to exercise cross-scenario refusals. Preserve named scenarios, or explicitly account for the narrower contract and prove that it covers the cases this repair is meant to support. [Allowed scenario names](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/DESIGN.md:27) · [Alternate scenario fixture](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/gate.py:338)\n    34\t\n    35\t- **The other §3 changes need sharper controls.** **3.1** adds no acceptance path. A closed list applied to every `what.period` would nevertheless exclude working values such as `rest of FY2027` and `period not specified`. Keep the list local to the operations that parse it. **3.3** would reject a correct calculation I confirmed passes today, `12% × 2x = 24%`. `share` would yield `0.24` with the same operands. The defect is multiplying a whole by a percentage as if the percentage were a raw multiplier. **3.5** excludes the spelling `USD trillion`, but the rung check still accepts `1.05` with an allowed unit and compares the raw number with billion-dollar bands. The enum does not itself verify magnitude or compatibility with the ladder. [Period parser](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/calc_check.py:285) · [Working period fixtures](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/gate.py:128) · [Product and share](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/calc_check.py:125) · [Rung comparison](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/calc_check.py:495)\n    36\t\n    37\t- **A1 and A2 miss cases that could decide the result.** The stored four-term residual, `$411B − $81.6B − $96.2B − $108B`, still fails C8 as a signed `sum`. I confirmed that omitting the relation fails and `time_aggregation` fails because the annual period overlaps the quarters. `stated_in_addition` passes even though it misdescribes the calculation. For A1, also carry a range outcome with a valid overlapping rung, a shared figure, a computed external reference, an unresolved answer, a roll-forward that records overlap, a valid scope bridge, both per-unit directions and a same-base calculation through the new wire shape. For A2, plant value plus range, a missing range end, duplicate member answers, conflicting reference markers, an outcome key mismatch, a shared-figure bypass, a wrong scenario, a forecast or estimate inside a reference, and a missing or wrong bridge. Test the actual cross-period share assumption too. Correct multiplication does not establish that a Q2 mix applies to a full year. Assert the intended refusal so a schema error cannot mask the check under test. [A1/A2](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/REPAIR-DESIGN.md:115) · [Stored residual](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/codex-r48.md:46) · [Sum compatibility](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/calc_check.py:407)\n    38\t\n    39\t- **The six requests test fewer than six independent evidence sets.** The joint pair and both separate requests intentionally share the same evidence. Each reuses **228 of 266** passage IDs from its corresponding paid request and **230 of 266** exact quotes appear somewhere in the paid plan. O reuses **30 of 30**. S has **9 of 50** exact quotes anywhere in the paid plan, including **6 of 50** in its corresponding paid request. E has no valid overlap figure as specified. A genuine Vera candidate, `old-3/9071`, reuses **15 of 15**, while `old-3/8753` reuses **9 of 9** by ID. The high-overlap cases are useful controls for the repair. The test needs a correctly identified request with new decisive evidence to claim it generalizes. Give each writer a fresh context per request. As written, each writer sees the entire batch and can carry its joint answer into the separate requests. [Input list and session setup](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/REPAIR-DESIGN.md:86) · [Evidence union](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/paid_gate.py:95)\n    40\t\n    41\t- **Part B can pass without demonstrating that a writer completed a usable calculation.** I ran an all-unresolved pair through the current checks. It returned zero errors and zero outcomes. Conversely, the design can call even **0 of 12** a clean conformance result if every refusal is attributed to the writer. The **6 of 12** warning has no stated basis and can hide a failure on every joint request. Register the forms each case can genuinely exercise, report supported forecasts, justified unresolved answers and refusals separately, and require successful coverage of the joint and singleton forms before calling the test clean. Have independent readers who did not write the design or the answers record two separate facts for each refusal: whether the exact outgoing request states the condition, and whether the answer is correct against the shown passages and governing design. Both problems can occur together. Resolve disagreements before declaring a pass. Classify truncation, session failure and checker exceptions separately from writer mistakes. Stronger writers can demonstrate that they can follow a single outgoing request. At $0, replay the observed Luna mistake patterns from both journals against the new schema and check that each is prevented or gets a clear reason, then compare strong writers on the old and repaired requests in separate contexts. Actual Luna compliance remains unmeasured. [Classification and stop rules](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/REPAIR-DESIGN.md:139) · [Unresolved branch](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/calc_check.py:479) · [Stored failure census](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/PAID-GATE-RESULT.md:25)\n    42\t\n    43\t## Build and record\n    44\t\n    45\t- **The rehearsal must read the new plan, and the old run must keep its reader.** The rehearsal loads `paid-plan.json` at import and validates outgoing bytes against that object. Updating stubs alone still rehearses the old schema. The equivalent repair run should consume a separately pinned plan and compare it with the old plan by case, evidence, questions, family data and intended request changes. The old journals store the raw old shape. Replacing `strip` everywhere would reinterpret those entries during packet rendering and recovery. Recovery also rewrites `results.json`. Bind decoding and checking to the plan version and verify historical reproduction on a copy. I confirmed the current plan hash matches its pin, the run’s plan has identical data, and the current builder recreates the exact original plan bytes. [Rehearsal plan](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/paid_rehearsal.py:18) · [Byte comparison](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/paid_rehearsal.py:120) · [Recovery and send pin](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/paid_gate.py:365)\n    46\t\n    47\t- **9,624 characters is one old schema’s size.** Across the 12 stored cases, schemas were **5,652–10,056 characters** and user messages **13,202–134,199 characters**. The 36 scored calls used **5,534–38,797 input tokens** each. Copying the evidence enum adds up to about **2,332 serialized characters** before new descriptions, and the enum already appears in `cites` and `pushes`. Use one schema definition for passage IDs if the strict request path accepts it. Report whole-request characters, estimated tokens and maximum size before and after. Write the actual field descriptions into the design for review now. Keep the paid examples as tests and use examples from other domains in the request. [Size proposal](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/REPAIR-DESIGN.md:167) · [Repeated passage enum](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/render_v13.py:61) · [Prompt size rule](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/AGENTS.md:220)\n    48\t\n    49\t**Recommendation:** bring back one revised design with the exact schema wording and corrected case list. The decisive $0 test is to send the registered good and bad forms through the same schema, converter and checks the gate uses, then read the blind writers’ first answers against those same rules and passages.     1\t# Round 49, Claude's reflection after Codex's design review (written before round 50)\n     2\t\n     3\t## What round 49 showed\n     4\t\n     5\t- **The repair is not bounded.** Codex walked all 58 refusal templates in `calc_check.py` (893 lines). Rules for C1 through C10 are\n     6\t  still unstated after the repair as designed: the component choice by form; C2's tolerance and bound direction; C3's\n     7\t  compare_periods; C4's path-through-the-forecast-node rule; C5's reference graph; C6's overlap semantics; C7's arity limits; C8's\n     8\t  operand rules per operation; C9's dependence test.\n     9\t- **Two of my four check changes are unsound.**\n    10\t  - The \"vs\" growth label lets the answer supply its own proof. A 12% sequential rate relabelled as year over year passes.\n    11\t  - The containment rule lets \"not be over $300 billion\" pass as \"> $300B\", and \"-12%\" pass as \"+12%\".\n    12\t- **My test inputs were wrong.** old-2 v41 is a Fed question, and S is CY2026, not CY2027. The pair and O reuse almost all of the\n    13\t  gate's evidence.\n    14\t- **Part B could \"pass\" with zero usable answers.** All-unresolved answers pass every check.\n    15\t\n    16\t## The pattern (AGENTS §1, §7.3: \"When one mechanism draws findings round after round, stop answering findings: propose one structural rule\")\n    17\t\n    18\t- The contract grew over 30 design rounds (46 to 46ad) and the 8 harness rounds. Most of its rules came from answering one gate\n    19\t  fixture's finding at a time: R1 to R4, G1 to G4, the relabel and route variants. `calc_check.py` carries 29 council-tagged rules.\n    20\t- Each rule is identity or arithmetic, as D725 asked. Together, though, they form a 58-way conjunction over about 20 figures per\n    21\t  answer.\n    22\t- **The rules were never written for the model.** The $0 gate tested them only against answers written by someone who knew them.\n    23\t- **Stating every rule** would turn the schema into a manual. It would also not change the conjunction: luna (medium effort, one pass)\n    24\t  must satisfy all of them at once. Round 48's reflection already warned that a long conjunction fails by chance even when each part\n    25\t  is usually right.\n    26\t- **What this means for D734's hypothesis.** The hypothesis was that \"a bounded repair (descriptions, enums and four check fixes)\n    27\t  makes the format statable\". Round 49 contradicts it. By D355, the line stops and goes back to Alex.\n    28\t\n    29\t## What still serves D725's goal\n    30\t\n    31\tD725's diagnosis was three things:\n    32\t- related questions have no shared, checked calculation;\n    33\t- a prose answer cannot be checked;\n    34\t- each question is judged alone, on split inputs.\n    35\t\n    36\tThe goal is that the numbers a member publishes are consistent with each other and with the passages. These checks carry that goal\n    37\tdirectly:\n    38\t1. A source number appears in the cited quote.\n    39\t2. Arithmetic is right.\n    40\t3. A part's outcome does not exceed its whole's.\n    41\t4. A shared quarter has one forecast that both members use.\n    42\t5. A rung contains its outcome.\n    43\t6. A reference is not the system's own estimate.\n    44\t\n    45\tThe rest are refinements: typed relations, bridges and `converts`, roll-forward formats, per-unit identities, scenario membership\n    46\talong paths, C9's perturbation test and the exact-expression grammar. Each was added to stop a case a reviewer constructed.\n    47\tArguably they are better shown to the readers as recorded assumptions than enforced as refusals: the design already records\n    48\toverlaps, adjustments and bridges for readers, not refusals.\n    49\t\n    50\t## Options for Alex (my lean, to be challenged in round 50)\n    51\t\n    52\t- **(A) Continue the full repair.** State all 58 rules and fix the unsound changes, which means several more council rounds. The risk\n    53\t  stays that luna cannot meet the conjunction even when every rule is stated.\n    54\t- **(B) Shrink the format to the core six checks** (above), with a short schema that states each one. The other structure is kept as\n    55\t  recorded assumptions for the readers, never as refusals. Then rebuild the $0 gate and the blind test on the smaller format.\n    56\t  - This is a new design step inside judging-v13, not a wording fix.\n    57\t  - It would be measured by the same 36-request gate, with D725's lines unchanged.\n    58\t- **(C) Close judging-v13.** poc-v118 stays the product, and the number answers stay a known defect.\n    59\t\n    60\tMy lean is (B). It is the structural rule: enforce only what the goal needs, state every enforced rule beside its field, and show the\n    61\trest to readers. What would refute it: the six checks cannot be stated in a short schema without the refinements creeping back, or a\n    62\tblind writer still needs unstated rules.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
